| Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: BOE portal status "ساري" for the Labor Law; the most recent amending instrument recorded on the portal for the articles read is M/44 of 8/2/1446H, in force 20/8/1446H (19 February 2025); no later amendment is recorded on the articles read. `docs/amendment-watch-2026-09-04.md` leads 1.1, 1.2 and 1.5 concern this file and are unconfirmed |

## Scope

This file identifies the points at which the Saudi labour-administration platforms are the legally required channel for an employer's obligation, so that a reviewer can distinguish "the contract is valid but undocumented" from "the employer is in breach". It covers corporate (private-sector) employers under the Labor Law: establishment registration, employment-contract documentation on Qiwa, work-permit issuance and renewal, occupation change, transfer of services (نقل خدمات) including the cases where a non-Saudi worker may move without the employer's consent, wage payment through the Wage Protection System via Mudad, GOSI registration and wage declarations, and exit/re-entry and final-exit visas. Musaned (domestic workers) is noted for completeness only; domestic workers are outside the Labor Law (Art. 7) and outside the scope of the consuming skills. The file does not cover platform fees, Qiwa subscription terms, or the Ministry of Interior's residency rules beyond what the Labor Law says.

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Labor Law Art. 15, 16 | On starting work in any establishment the employer must notify the competent labour office in writing of: the establishment's name, type, head office and correspondence address; the licensed economic activity with commercial-register or licence number, date and issuer (copy attached); the number of workers to be employed; the name of the responsible manager; any other data the ministry requires. The employer must designate a responsible representative at the workplace where it cannot act itself, notify the labour office of the partner's or manager's name, and notify any change within seven days. | على صاحب العمل عند بدء العمل في أي منشأة أن يبلغ مكتب العمل المختص كتابة بالبيانات الآتية … اسم المنشأة ونوعها ومركزها … النشاط الاقتصادي المرخص له بمزاولته مع ذكر رقم السجل التجاري … عدد العمال المراد تشغيلهم … اسم مدير المنشأة المسؤول … عليه في حالة تغييره أن يبلغ المكتب باسم الشريك أو المدير الجديد خلال سبعة أيام على الأكثر. | The "labour office file" is now the Qiwa establishment account (ملف المنشأة), opened against the commercial register and used for every later platform step; the seven-day manager-change notice is done by updating the establishment's authorised manager on Qiwa `[model knowledge — verify]`. In diligence, an establishment number that does not exist on Qiwa, or a listed manager who has left, is an Art. 15-16 breach and a practical block on all HRSD services. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Labor Law Art. 51 (as amended by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 18(2) | The employment contract must be written in two copies, one kept by each party, and must be documented in accordance with the relevant statutory provisions and as the Regulation sets out; the contract exists even if unwritten, in which case the worker alone may prove it and his rights by all means of proof, and either party may demand a written contract at any time. Regulation Art. 18(2): the employer must document workers' contracts electronically through the ministry's approved platform. | يجب أن يكتب عقد العمل من نسختين، يحتفظ كل من طرفيه بنسخة منه، ويجب توثيقه وفق الأحكام النظامية ذات الصلة وفي ضوء ما تحدده اللائحة، ويعد العقد قائمًا ولو كان غير مكتوب … [اللائحة، المادة 18] يجب على صاحب العمل توثيق عقود العاملين إلكترونيًا عبر المنصة المعتمدة لدى الوزارة. | Qiwa authentication of every employee's contract is a legal duty on the employer, not a convenience: the amended Art. 51 says "يجب توثيقه" and the Regulation names the platform. An undocumented contract remains valid and enforceable against the employer (the worker can prove it by any means); the sanction falls on the employer: Art. 229 (fine up to SAR 100,000 per person, closure) applied through the Minister's schedule under Art. 230 (the current schedule amount is `[model knowledge — verify]`), and loss of Nitaqat credit for undocumented Saudi contracts from April 2026 `[model knowledge — verify]`, see `saudization-nitaqat.md`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 51; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 18 |
| Labor Law Art. 52 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H, para (1) as reworded by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 18(1) | The ministry issues a unified template for each type of employment contract containing at least: the employer's name and place; the worker's name, nationality, identity data and residential address; the agreed wage including benefits and allowances; the type and place of work; the joining date; the term if fixed; and each party's basic rights and obligations (subject to Art. 37 for non-Saudis). The contract must follow the template; the parties may add clauses that do not conflict with the Law, the Regulation and the decisions issued under it. Regulation Art. 18(1) adopts the unified templates in Annex 5. | تضع الوزارة نموذجًا موحدًا لكل نوع من أنواع عقد العمل … يجب أن يكون عقد العمل وفق النموذج المشار إليه … ولطرفي العقد أن يضيفا إليه بنودًا أخرى، بما لا يتعارض مع أحكام هذا النظام ولائحته. تعتمد النماذج الموحدة لعقد العمل بأنواعه، بالصيغ المرافقة لهذه اللائحة (الملحق رقم 5). | The Qiwa contract is the unified template; a bespoke English-language contract is permissible only as additional clauses attached to (and not conflicting with) the Qiwa template. Where the Qiwa contract and a side letter differ on wage, term or notice, expect the labour court to apply whichever is more favourable to the worker and to treat the Qiwa wage as the declared wage for WPS and GOSI `[model knowledge — verify]`. Hiring-review should check the Qiwa contract, not only the offer letter. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 52; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 18 |
| Labor Law Art. 37 (as replaced by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 12 | A non-Saudi's employment contract must be written and fixed-term; if it states no term, its term is one year from the worker's actual start date, and if work continues after that year it is deemed renewed for a similar period. Regulation: a non-Saudi's contract is fixed-term however long its term or renewals, and never becomes indefinite even if the parties so agree. (The original rule that the work-permit term filled the gap is no longer in force.) | يجب أن يكون عقد عمل غير السعودي مكتوباً ومحدد المدة. وإذا خلا العقد من بيان مدته تعد مدته (سنة) من تاريخ مباشرة العامل الفعلية، وإذا استمر العمل بعد انتهاء هذه المدة، عُدَّ متجدداً لمدة مماثلة … [اللائحة] يعتبر عقد عمل غير السعودي محدد المدة مهما طالت مدته، أو مدد تجديده، وفي جميع الأحوال لا يتحول إلى عقد غير محدد المدة حتى لو اتفق الطرفان على ذلك. | On Qiwa an expatriate's contract is entered with a term; where none is stated the statutory default is one year from actual start, rolling annually. Termination-review must treat every expatriate contract as fixed-term for Art. 77 compensation purposes regardless of what the parties wrote, and must compute the unexpired term from the actual start date, not the permit date. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 37; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 12 |
| Labor Law Art. 33, 34; Art. 35 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H and M/44 of 1446H) | A non-Saudi may not work, and may not be allowed to work, without a work permit from the ministry on its form, granted only if he entered lawfully with work authorisation, has needed qualifications not available among Saudis (or is needed ordinary labour), and is contracted with and under the responsibility of an employer. No other licence substitutes for the work permit. The ministry may, for considerations it assesses, refuse to renew a work permit where the employer has breached the ministry's job-localisation standards or any other requirement or control in the Regulation; the Regulation sets procedures so the worker is not harmed by non-renewal, including transfer of his services to another employer without the violating employer's consent. | لا يجوز لغير السعودي أن يمارس عملًا، ولا يجوز أن يسمح له بمزاولته، إلا بعد الحصول على رخصة عمل من الوزارة … لا يغني عن رخصة العمل المشار إليها أي تصريح أو ترخيص يكون مطلوبًا من جهة أخرى … للوزارة -لاعتبارات تقدرها- أن تمتنع عن تجديد رخصة العمل متى خالف صاحب العمل المعايير الخاصة بتوطين الوظائف التي تضعها الوزارة، وأيًّا من الاشتراطات أو الضوابط الأخرى الواردة في اللائحة … بما في ذلك إمكانية نقل خدمة العامل إلى صاحب عمل آخر دون موافقة صاحب العمل المخالف. | Work permits are issued and renewed on Qiwa (annually, against the work-permit fee and the expatriate levy, and only if the entity's Nitaqat band allows it) and the iqama is then issued or renewed on Muqeem/Absher against the valid work permit `[model knowledge — verify]`. Art. 35 is the statutory hook for the Nitaqat renewal block and for the worker's exit route when the employer is non-compliant (Regulation Art. 14 Second item 6). Employing an expatriate whose permit has lapsed is an Art. 33 breach penalised per person under Art. 229 and blocks recruitment and transfers for the whole establishment (Regulation Art. 14 First item 12(e), Second item 2). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Labor Law Art. 38 with Regulation Art. 14 Third | An employer may not employ a worker in an occupation other than that on his work permit, and the worker may not work outside his occupation before completing the statutory occupation-change procedure. Regulation: occupation change requires a valid licence or commercial register registered with the labour office and an active business; the new occupation named per the Saudi occupational classification; certified qualifications (with two exceptions); the establishment's Nitaqat ratio achieved; not to a Saudi-only occupation or one whose change is barred; application through the ministry's approved platform; and the consent of any sector regulator where the occupation is licensed. | لا يجوز لصاحب العمل توظيف العامل في مهنة غير المهنة المدونة في رخصة عمله، ويحظر على العامل الاشتغال في غير مهنته قبل اتخاذ الإجراءات النظامية لتغيير المهنة … يقدم طلب تغيير المهنة عن طريق المنصة المعتمدة لدى الوزارة. | Job-title mismatches between the Qiwa/iqama occupation and the actual role are among the most common findings in a Saudi workforce audit; each is an Art. 38 breach penalised per person under Art. 229 through the Minister's schedule and, for licensed professions (engineering, accounting, health), a sector-regulator issue too. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 38; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for the Regulation |
| Labor Law Art. 39 (as replaced by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 13; Art. 30 (as replaced by M/44) with Art. 229 bis | Without following the prescribed rules and procedures, an employer may not let its worker work for others or for his own account, a worker may not work for another employer or for his own account, and an employer may not employ another's worker; the ministry inspects establishments, records violations and applies the Law's penalties, referring matters within the Ministry of Interior's competence to it, and the Ministry of Interior refers violating employers it detects to HRSD for penalties. Regulation Art. 13: a non-Saudi whose work the ministry approves through the Ajeer program is not in breach. Art. 30 as replaced: no natural or legal person may carry on the activity of employing Saudis, recruiting workers, or "outsourcing activity" (نشاط الإسناد) without an HRSD licence; the Regulation sets the licensing conditions and licensee obligations. Art. 229 bis (added by M/44): breach of Art. 30(1) is fined SAR 200,000 to 500,000, without prejudice to Art. 229. (The original Art. 233 fine of SAR 5,000-20,000 with deportation at the employer's expense is no longer in force; removal of the worker now runs through the Ministry of Interior referral.) | لا يجوز -بغير اتباع القواعد والإجراءات النظامية المقررة- أن يترك صاحب العمل عامله يعمل لدى الغير أو يعمل لحسابه الخاص، ولا يجوز للعامل أن يعمل لدى صاحب عمل آخر أو لحسابه الخاص، ولا يجوز لصاحب العمل توظيف عامل غيره، وتتولى الوزارة التفتيش على المنشآت، وضبط مخالفات هذا النظام … ومن ثم إحالة ما يدخل في اختصاص وزارة الداخلية إليها … لا يجوز لأي شخص طبيعي أو اعتباري أن يمارس نشاط توظيف السعوديين أو نشاط استقدام العمال أو نشاط الإسناد ما لم يكن مرخصاً له بذلك من الوزارة … يعاقب بغرامة لا تقل عن (200,000) مائتي ألف ريال ولا تزيد على (500,000) خمسمائة ألف ريال؛ كل من يخالف أحكام الفقرة (1) من المادة (الثلاثين). | Intra-group secondment of an expatriate between two Saudi entities, or the use of another company's staff on site, is lawful only through Ajeer (temporary work notices on Qiwa) or a formal transfer of services; informal arrangements expose both companies to Art. 229 penalties and the worker to removal via the Ministry of Interior. Since M/44, supplying workers to third parties is a licensed "outsourcing activity" under Art. 30, and doing it unlicensed carries the SAR 200,000-500,000 fine of Art. 229 bis, which is the largest fixed fine in the Law. Worker-classification and vendor-agreement reviews should ask for the Ajeer notice for any on-site third-party expatriate and for the supplier's Art. 30 licence. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Arts. 30, 39, 229 bis; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 13 |
| Labor Law Art. 40 (para (1) as reworded by M/44 of 1446H) | The employer bears the non-Saudi worker's recruitment fees, iqama and work-permit fees and their renewal, the fines for delay in those that the employer causes, occupation-change fees, exit and re-entry fees, and the return ticket after the relationship ends; the worker bears his return cost if unfit for work or if he wishes to return without a legitimate reason. The employer bears the transfer-of-services fee for a worker who wishes to transfer to it. The employer bears the cost of preparing and repatriating a deceased worker's body unless GOSI does. | يتحمل صاحب العمل رسوم استقدام العامل غير السعودي، ورسوم الإقامة ورخصة العمل وتجديدهما، وما يترتب على تأخير ذلك من غرامات يتسبب بها صاحب العمل، ورسوم تغيير المهنة، والخروج والعودة، وتذكرة عودة العامل إلى موطنه بعد انتهاء العلاقة بين الطرفين … يتحمل صاحب العمل رسوم نقل خدمات العامل الذي يرغب في نقل خدماته إليه. | Every platform fee in this file (Qiwa work permit and levy, Muqeem iqama, Absher exit/re-entry, transfer fee) is by law an employer cost; deduction from the worker's wage or clawback at exit is unlawful, and clauses purporting to do so are a standard hiring-review and termination-review finding. The M/44 rewording limits the employer's liability for delay fines to those "caused by the employer", which opens a narrow argument that a fine caused by the worker (for example, failing to present for iqama renewal) may be charged to him; the only other costs the worker may bear are his own return ticket in the two Art. 40(2) cases. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Regulation Art. 14 First items 10, 12, 16 (recruitment) | Recruitment applications are submitted through the ministry's approved platform in accordance with Nitaqat. The ministry may refuse where the establishment has a collective delay in paying wages, proven commercial concealment, workers left to work for others or themselves, an unmet minimum Saudization ratio, has given false data (all recruitment stopped for up to five years, visas cancelled, no permits for workers recruited on them, criminal referral for forgery), has sold visas (five-year stop), has not renewed its workers' permits or iqamas, or in any other case Nitaqat provides. No worker may be recruited or transferred without actual work existing with the employer. | تقدم طلبات الاستقدام عن طريق المنصة المعتمدة لدى الوزارة … للوزارة رفض طلب الاستقدام في الحالات التالية: إذا تبين وجود تأخر جماعي في دفع الأجور، أو ثبوت التستر التجاري … إذا لم تجدد المنشأة رخص العمل أو الإقامة للعمالة الموجودة لديها … لا يجوز استقدام أو نقل خدمات العامل دون وجود عمل فعلي لدى صاحب العمل. | Visa issuance on Qiwa is conditioned on WPS compliance (collective wage delay), permit renewal discipline, and Nitaqat; a target's five-year recruitment suspension for false data is a material diligence finding. The "no recruitment without actual work" rule is the legal basis for the tasattur (visa trading) enforcement. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Regulation Art. 14 Second items 1, 11, 14, 15, 16, 17 (transfer of services with consent) | A non-Saudi worker may move to another employer without any minimum service period if the current employer consents, unless Nitaqat provides otherwise. Transfer requests are submitted electronically through the ministry's approved platform. Both the former and the new employer must keep the worker's written consent to the transfer before the procedure. The current employer's consent must be given electronically on the transfer request or by a paper waiver attested by an official body addressed to the requesting employer, and may not be made subject to any condition; the consent is not required in the no-consent cases below or for workers moving to a new contractor under continuing government operation, maintenance and cleaning contracts. If the transfer is not approved after the consent was given, the worker stays with the original employer. No decision or final judgment inconsistent with the transfer may exist before it is completed. | للعامل غير السعودي الانتقال لصاحب عمل آخر دون اشتراط توفر مدة محددة في حال موافقة صاحب العمل الحالي … تقدم طلبات نقل الخدمات لغرض العمل إلكترونيًا عن طريق المنصة المعتمدة لدى الوزارة … يجب توفر موافقة إلكترونية من صاحب العمل على طلب نقل خدمات العامل أو تنازل ورقي مصادق عليه من جهة رسمية … ولا يجوز تقييده بأي شرط. | The transfer runs on Qiwa: the receiving employer requests, the current employer accepts electronically, the worker accepts. "May not be conditioned" bars the practice of demanding payment or a release as the price of a Qiwa release; a clause in a settlement agreement conditioning the transfer approval on a waiver is unenforceable as a condition of the approval itself `[model knowledge — verify]` for court practice. Keep the worker's written consent on file (item 14). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Regulation Art. 14 Second items 5, 6, 7, 8, 18, 21 (transfer without the employer's consent) | A non-Saudi may transfer without the current employer's consent: (5) as a newly arrived worker for whom no work permit has been issued; (6) where his work permit or iqama has expired; (7) on expiry of the documented employment contract (subject to item 21); (8) by decision of the Minister or delegate where the employer prolongs a pending court case (absence from two hearings without accepted excuse, or proven delay), on the court's recommendation, where the establishment has not paid three consecutive months' wages or is late past the third month's due date (application within one Hijri year), where the employer is absent (travel, imprisonment, death or otherwise) leaving the establishment unable to meet its obligations for three months without an appointed representative, for categories or nationalities with special instructions on expiry or employer termination, for an accompanying dependant after the end of the relationship, for spouse reunification, where the worker reports commercial concealment with evidence, or in any other public-interest case the Minister decides. If a transfer in an item (7) case is not approved, the Minister may license the worker to work for another employer. (21) Subject to Art. 77, a non-Saudi may move without consent once he has completed twelve months from entry and has given the current employer not less than ninety days' notice before ending the relationship, unless otherwise agreed. Item 2 bars transfers to an establishment with unrenewed permits or iqamas, or a worker three months in the Kingdom without a permit; item 4 bars transfers to an employer with collective wage delay, tasattur, or workers left to work for others. | يجوز نقل خدمات العامل غير السعودي الجديد والذي لم تصدر له رخصة عمل دون موافقة صاحب العمل … يجوز نقل خدمات العامل غير السعودي الذي انتهت رخصة عمله أو إقامته دون موافقة صاحب العمل الحالي … للعامل غير السعودي الانتقال لصاحب عمل آخر عند انتهاء عقد العمل الموثق دون موافقة صاحب العمل الحالي … إذا لم تقم المنشأة بدفع أجور العامل المستحقة لثلاثة أشهر متتالية … مع مراعاة أحكام المادة (السابعة والسبعون) من النظام، للعامل غير السعودي الانتقال لصاحب العمل آخر دون اشتراط موافقة صاحب العمل الحالي عند توفر الشروط الآتية: أن يكون أمضى اثنا عشر شهرًا له من تاريخ دخوله للمملكة. أن يقوم العامل بإشعار صاحب العمل الحالي قبل الانتقال بمدة لا تقل عن (تسعين) يومًا. | This is the 2021 "labour mobility" reform as now codified in the Regulation: an expatriate can leave at contract expiry, or after twelve months in the Kingdom on ninety days' notice, and Qiwa processes the transfer without the employer's release. The employer's protection is Art. 77 (compensation for early termination of a fixed-term contract, preserved by item 21) and the Qiwa contract term. The three-months-unpaid-wages ground links directly to WPS data on Mudad. Item 2 and item 4 mean a receiving employer with lapsed permits or WPS arrears cannot take anyone in, whatever the worker's rights. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Regulation Art. 14 Second items 19, 20 (corporate changes are not transfers) | The following are name amendments, not transfers of services: continuation of a deceased owner's business by heirs (with power of attorney or conversion to a company, Ministry of Commerce approval); change of the establishment's name, objects, all or some partners, or company type with the commercial register retained (MoC approval); merger of two or more establishments whether or not the legal entity changes, stated in the constitutional document (MoC approval); separation of a partner with a share of activities as a sole business (MoC approval); conversion of a sole establishment into a company by admission of partners; transfer of ownership of the establishment with all workers continuing and moving to the new owner with the register retained (MoC approval). Moving non-Saudi workforce registration between branches of the same entity (without limit) or between entities of the same establishment is a workforce-registration transfer, not a transfer of services, under Nitaqat. | لا تعتبر الحالات التالية حالات نقل خدمات وإنما حالات تعديل مسمى وهي: … الاندماج بين منشأتين، أو أكثر سواء تغير الكيان القانوني، أو لم يتغير … نقل ملكية المنشأة مع استمرار جميع العاملين بالعمل فيها، وتحولهم للمالك الجديد مع بقاء سجلها التجاري … لا تعتبر الحالات التالية حالات نقل خدمات؛ وإنما حالات نقل تسجيل قوى عاملة: نقل تسجيل القوى العاملة من غير السعوديين بين الفروع في نفس الكيان. | For M&A: a share deal, a merger, or a business transfer with the commercial register retained does not require individual Qiwa transfers or the workers' consent under item 14, and does not consume the buyer's Nitaqat headroom; an asset deal into a new commercial register does, worker by worker, with each expatriate's consent and the receiving entity's Nitaqat band as gates. Structure the deal with this table in view and put the Qiwa "name amendment" step on the closing checklist. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Labor Law Art. 90 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H) | Wages and all sums due to the worker must be paid in the official currency: daily-paid workers at least weekly, monthly-paid workers monthly, piece-work with weekly instalments proportionate to work done and the balance within the week after delivery, others at least weekly. Establishments are obliged to pay wages into the workers' accounts through banks approved in the Kingdom, provided the due dates above are not exceeded; the Minister may exempt some establishments. (The original text, which made bank payment optional with the worker's consent and required payment at the workplace during working hours, is no longer in force.) | يجب دفع أجر العامل وكل مبلغ مستحق له بالعملة الرسمية للبلاد طبقاً للأحكام الآتية … العمال ذوو الأجور الشهرية: تصرف أجورهم مرة في الشهر … تلزم المنشآت بدفع الأجور في حسابات العمال عن طريق البنوك المعتمدة في المملكة، بشرط ألا يتجاوز موعد استحقاقها المواعيد المحددة أعلاه، ويجوز للوزير استثناء بعض المنشآت من ذلك. | Bank payment of wages is a statutory duty since 2015, not a WPS convenience: cash wages, or wages paid after the monthly due date, breach Art. 90 regardless of the Wage Protection System, and are penalised under Art. 229 through the Minister's schedule (the original Art. 235 fine of SAR 500-3,000 per worker is no longer in force). The WPS adds the monthly reporting and matching layer (next two rows). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Regulation, Model Work-Organisation Regulation Art. 15 (Annex to the Regulation) | Subject to any procedures or arrangements the Wage Protection Program provides, workers' wages are paid in the official currency on a fixed date each month and deposited into the workers' accounts through banks approved in the Kingdom. (The model regulation is the mandatory template for the employer's work-organisation regulation under Labor Law Art. 12; establishments with fewer than fifty workers may adopt it as is.) | مع مراعاة أي إجراءات، أو ترتيبات ينص عليها برنامج حماية الأجور؛ تدفع أجور العمال بالعملة الرسمية للبلاد في تاريخ …/…/… وتودع في حسابات العمال عن طريق البنوك المعتمدة في المملكة. | This is the only reference to the Wage Protection Program in the primary texts read: it presupposes the program and folds the Art. 90(2) bank-deposit duty into every employer's internal regulation. The WPS itself (monthly wage file uploaded through Mudad or a bank, matched against Qiwa and GOSI data, compliance percentage thresholds, service suspension and fines for non-compliance) rests on HRSD ministerial decisions not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Wage Protection System via Mudad (HRSD ministerial decisions, not in the source set) | Every private-sector establishment must upload a monthly wage file covering all workers (Saudi and non-Saudi) through Mudad or an approved bank, showing the wage per worker matched to the Qiwa contract and GOSI declaration; HRSD computes a compliance percentage and, below a threshold (80% in the phased rollout) or after a defined period of non-upload, suspends Qiwa services (visas, transfers, permit renewals) and applies the violations schedule; deductions above the Art. 92 limits and unexplained variances are flagged. Establishments with very few workers were phased in last. Non-payment for three consecutive months is also a transfer-without-consent ground (row above, settled). | (برنامج حماية الأجور: رفع ملف الأجور شهرياً عبر منصة مدد، ومطابقة الأجور مع بيانات قوى والتأمينات، وإيقاف الخدمات عند تدني نسبة الالتزام) | WPS compliance is the single most consequential platform duty in practice: a lapse blocks visas and transfers for the whole establishment and is used as evidence in wage claims. Diligence should request twelve months of Mudad compliance reports and reconcile them to payroll. The thresholds, decision numbers and current fine amounts must be confirmed from HRSD. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Wage-clause direct enforcement (MHRSD-MoJ framework; amendment-watch lead 1.1) | Under a joint HRSD-Ministry of Justice framework the wage clause of a Qiwa-documented employment contract is treated as an enforceable instrument: a worker (or employer) can apply to the Enforcement Court through the Qiwa-Najiz-Mudad link for unpaid documented wages without a labour-court judgment. Phase 3 reportedly extends this to open-ended contracts from 6 August 2026. | (اعتبار بند الأجر في عقد العمل الموثق سنداً تنفيذياً عبر الربط بين قوى وناجز ومدد) | If confirmed, Qiwa documentation plus Mudad wage records convert unpaid wages into a directly enforceable claim, which raises the stakes of accurate Qiwa wage entries and timely WPS uploads. Source: King & Spalding note in the amendment watch; unconfirmed on hrsd.gov.sa or moj.gov.sa. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Regulation, flexible-work rules Second items 14, 15 | A flexible-work (hourly, Saudi-only) contract must be electronic and fixed-term with an hourly wage; employing a flexible-work worker requires the contract to be documented on the electronic portal the ministry designates, and anyone employing a flexible-work worker without a documented contract is liable to the penalties in Ministerial Decision No. 178743 of 27/9/1440H as amended. | يشترط لتوظيف العاملين بنظام (العمل المرن) توثيق العقد في البوابة الإلكترونية التي تحددها الوزارة، ويعاقب كل من يشغل عامل بنظام (العمل المرن) بدون عقد عمل موثق بالعقوبات الواردة في القرار الوزاري رقم 178743 وتاريخ 1440/9/27هـ. | This is the one place the Regulation itself names the penalties decision for undocumented contracts (MD 178743 of 1440H is the HRSD violations and penalties schedule issued under Labor Law Art. 230(2)-(3) `[model knowledge — verify]` for the identification, reportedly revised from 25 February 2026). Flexible-work contracts are documented on the flexible-work portal, not the standard Qiwa contract flow `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Social Insurance Law Arts. 7, 8(4), 9, 10 (GOSI online) | The employer must register its establishment and each contributor with GOSI within the Regulation's grace period, submit wage data in the manner GOSI sets, and pay both shares of contributions within the Regulation's period (2% per month delay fine); government bodies and state-controlled companies must demand a GOSI certificate before paying dues, awarding tenders, amending or renewing the commercial register, licensing, or approving recruitment from abroad. See `social-insurance-law.md`. | تحدد اللائحة … مهلة لأصحاب العمل للاشتراك عن المشتركين … يجب على صاحب العمل أن يقدم إلى المؤسسة -وفق الطريقة التي تحددها– بيانات بالأجور أو الرواتب الخاضعة للاشتراك … مطالبةُ صاحب العمل الذي يتعامل معها بأن يقدم شهادة صادرة من المؤسسة. | Registration, monthly wage updates and payment run through the GOSI online portal, which is integrated with Qiwa (a new Qiwa contract is expected to match a GOSI registration at the same wage) and with the Ministry of Commerce (commercial-register renewal checks GOSI compliance) `[model knowledge — verify]` for the integrations. The statutory duty and the certificate rule are settled. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Exit/re-entry and final-exit visas (Absher / Muqeem; Labor Law Art. 40) | The Labor Law fixes only that the employer bears exit and re-entry fees and the return ticket at the end of the relationship (Art. 40). The visas themselves are Ministry of Interior residency acts issued on Absher (business) or Muqeem: single or multiple exit/re-entry during employment, and a final-exit visa (خروج نهائي) when the relationship ends, after which the iqama is cancelled. Since the 2021 labour-mobility reform an expatriate can request exit/re-entry and final exit himself through Absher with notice to the employer, removing the employer's veto. | (رسوم الخروج والعودة على صاحب العمل - المادة الأربعون؛ إصدار تأشيرات الخروج والعودة والخروج النهائي عبر أبشر أعمال ومقيم) | Termination-review: the final-exit visa is the operational end of an expatriate's employment and is normally issued after the end-of-service settlement; issuing it before settlement, or withholding it after settlement, are both common dispute triggers. Only the fee-allocation in Art. 40 is settled; the Absher/Muqeem mechanics and the worker-initiated exit rule are practice points to verify. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 40 fees; `[model knowledge — verify]` for the Absher/Muqeem mechanics and worker-initiated exit |
| Musaned (domestic workers; note only) | The Regulation's rules for licensed recruitment and labour-services companies define the "approved platform" as the electronic platform through which licensees' operations are documented, "such as Musaned and Ajeer Recruitment and any platform the ministry approves", and require licensees to conclude and document the worker's contract through the approved platform; providing labour services without documenting them through the Ajeer Recruitment platform is a listed violation. Domestic workers are excluded from the Labor Law (Art. 7) and governed by the Domestic Workers Regulation; corporate employers encounter Musaned only when sponsoring household staff for executives. | المنصة المعتمدة: هي منصة إلكترونية يتم من خلالها توثيق عمليات المرخص لهم مثل "مساند، وأجير استقدام وأي منصة تعتمدها الوزارة" … ثبوت تقديم خدمات عمالية دون توثيقها عبر منصة أجير استقدام. | Out of scope for the consuming skills. If an executive's employment package includes a company-sponsored domestic worker, the contract and visa run on Musaned under the Domestic Workers Regulation, not on Qiwa, and the company should not be the sponsor of record `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for the definitional reference; the rest `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Labor Law Arts. 229, 230, 231, 232, 233 (penalties chapter as restructured by M/46 of 1436H; Art. 230(5) as amended by M/44 of 1446H) | Without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law, any breach of the Law, its Regulation or the decisions under it is punishable by one or more of: a fine up to SAR 100,000; closure up to 30 days; permanent closure; the penalty may be doubled on repetition and fines multiply by the number of persons concerned (Art. 229). The ministry, by decision of the Minister or his delegate, may impose the fine and temporary closure up to half the maximum, with appeal to the competent administrative court; the Minister issues a graduated schedule of violations and penalties within that half, and a second schedule of violations exceeding it or warranting permanent closure, which the ministry must take to the competent court; the ministry and the violator may settle by payment of the fine the ministry assesses, by decision of the Minister or his delegate (Art. 230). The violator must remove the violation within the period the Regulation sets, failing which it is a new violation (Art. 231). Fines are collected as public funds and go to the Human Resources Development Fund (Art. 232). The Minister may reward anyone who helps uncover a violation with up to 25% of the fine collected (Art. 233). Arts. 234-235 (added by M/14 of 1440H): labour claims are time-barred twelve months after the end of the relationship absent an accepted excuse or an admission; the employer may not change the working conditions to the worker's detriment while a case is pending. (The original Arts. 230-241, with fixed fines per article such as SAR 2,000-5,000 per worker for work-permit, occupation and expatriate-contract breaches, are no longer in force.) | يعاقب كل من يخل بأي حكم من أحكام هذا النظام أو لائحته أو القرارات الصادرة تنفيذا له، بعقوبة أو أكثر من العقوبات الآتية: أ – غرامة مالية لا تتجاوز مائة ألف ريـال. ب – إغلاق المنشأة لمدة لا تزيد على ثلاثين يوماً. ج – إغلاق المنشأة نهائيًّا … تتعدد الغرامات بتعدد الأشخاص الذين وقعت المخالفة في شأنهم … للوزارة – بقرار من الوزير أو من ينيبه – إيقاع العقوبتين أو إحداهما … بما لا يتجاوز نصف الحد الأعلى المقرر لأي منهما، ويجوز التظلم من القرار الصادر بالعقوبة أمام المحكمة الإدارية المختصة … يلزم مرتكب المخالفة بإزالة المخالفة خلال مهلة تحددها اللائحة، وفي حالة عدم إزالتها تعد مخالفة جديدة. | Every platform failure in this file (no work permit, wrong occupation, no fixed-term expatriate contract, unnotified manager change, undocumented contract, cash wages) is punished under the same Art. 229 ceiling of SAR 100,000 per person concerned, with the actual amount taken from the Minister's schedule under Art. 230(2) (HRSD applies size-graduated amounts; the schedule in force, reportedly revised 25 February 2026, was not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`). Ministry-imposed penalties are capped at SAR 50,000 per person (half the maximum); anything above, or permanent closure, needs a court. The 25% informant reward (Art. 233) makes undocumented or cash-paid workers a live reporting risk. The operative commercial sanction remains the Qiwa service block. The Arabic file shows renumbering by M/46: the removal duty is "Art. 231 currently (formerly Art. 240)" and the Fund allocation "Art. 232 currently (formerly Art. 242)". | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- **The HRSD schedule of violations and penalties under Art. 230(2)-(3)** in force in 2026 (MD 178743 of 1440H as revised, reportedly from 25 February 2026): the graduated amounts for undocumented contracts, expired permits, occupation mismatch, cash wages and WPS non-compliance, and which violations sit in the court-only schedule under Art. 230(3).
- **Art. 35 as amended: the Regulation procedures "ensuring the worker is not harmed"** by a refusal to renew, and how Qiwa operationalises the no-consent transfer for workers of a non-compliant employer.
- **Art. 30 as amended: the scope of "outsourcing activity" (نشاط الإسناد)** requiring an HRSD licence, and whether intra-group secondments or client-site placements by ordinary companies fall within it (Art. 229 bis exposure of SAR 200,000-500,000).
- **The instrument that makes Qiwa the "approved platform"** for contract documentation, transfers, occupation changes and recruitment: the Regulation says "المنصة المعتمدة لدى الوزارة" without naming Qiwa; confirm the HRSD decision that designates it and the deadline for documenting contracts of employees hired before the Regulation.
- **WPS decisions**: the current HRSD decision(s) establishing the Wage Protection Program, the compliance-percentage thresholds, the grace periods for non-upload, the current fine amounts, and the treatment of employees on unpaid leave, secondment or notice.
- **Penalties schedule**: the ministerial decision in force (MD 178743 of 1440H as revised, reportedly from 25 February 2026) and its size-graduated amounts for undocumented contracts, expired permits, occupation mismatch and WPS non-compliance.
- **Wage-clause direct enforcement** (phase 3 from 6 August 2026, as claimed): confirm the joint HRSD-MoJ framework, which contract types are covered, and whether the employer can use it against the worker.
- **Transfer-without-consent practice**: whether Qiwa applies the twelve-month / ninety-day route (Regulation Art. 14 Second item 21) automatically or requires a ministry decision; whether Art. 77 compensation is enforced against a transferring worker in practice; whether item 15's "may not be conditioned" defeats settlement agreements that trade the release for a waiver.
- **Absher/Muqeem rules** on worker-initiated exit/re-entry and final exit, and the employer's notice rights.
- **Integration effects**: what a Qiwa-GOSI wage mismatch triggers, and whether the Ministry of Commerce blocks commercial-register renewal for GOSI or WPS non-compliance automatically.
- **Musaned/Domestic Workers Regulation**: confirmed out of scope; no question unless an executive package includes household staff.

## How skills should use this file

- `hiring-review` (employment-legal), at the contract-form and authorisation steps: cite Art. 51 (as amended, via the Regulation) and Regulation Art. 18 for the Qiwa documentation duty and unified template; Art. 37 with Regulation Art. 12 for the fixed-term rule for non-Saudis; Arts. 33-35 for the work permit; Art. 40 for fee allocation (any clause shifting permit, iqama, exit or transfer fees to the worker is a finding). The WPS row and the Absher/Muqeem row are `[model knowledge — verify]` and must be flagged as such.
- `termination-review` (employment-legal), at the exit-mechanics step: cite Regulation Art. 14 Second items 5-8 and 21 (worker's right to transfer without consent, ninety-day notice, three-months-unpaid-wages ground) and item 15 (release may not be conditioned) when reviewing a separation agreement with an expatriate; cite Art. 40 for the return ticket and exit fees; cite Art. 90 (as replaced by M/46) for the timing and bank payment of final wages, with Art. 229 as the penalty. The final-exit mechanics are `[model knowledge — verify]`.
- `worker-classification` (employment-legal): cite Art. 39 (as replaced by M/44) with Regulation Art. 13 (Ajeer), Art. 30 (licensed outsourcing activity) and Art. 229 bis when a non-Saudi is working on a client's site or for a group affiliate; cite Regulation Art. 14 First item 16 (no recruitment or transfer without actual work). A contractor or secondee arrangement without an Ajeer notice or a transfer is an Art. 39 finding.
- `entity-compliance` (corporate-legal): list Qiwa establishment file and authorised manager (Arts. 15-16), contract documentation status (Art. 51 / Regulation Art. 18), work-permit expiry calendar (Art. 33), WPS upload status (`[model knowledge — verify]`), and GOSI registration and payment (Social Insurance Law Arts. 7-10) as recurring items.
- `diligence-issue-extraction` and `tabular-review` (corporate-legal): for each Saudi entity extract the Qiwa establishment status and Nitaqat band, the count of undocumented contracts, expired work permits and occupation mismatches, twelve months of WPS compliance, any recruitment suspension (Regulation Art. 14 First item 12), and the deal structure's treatment under Regulation Art. 14 Second items 19-20 (name amendment versus worker-by-worker transfer). Route asset-deal workforce transfers to the closing checklist with the receiving entity's Nitaqat band as a condition.
- **Computed numbers.** The statutory exposure is Art. 229: up to SAR 100,000 per person concerned (up to SAR 50,000 per person if imposed by the ministry rather than a court, Art. 230(1)), doubled on repetition, plus SAR 200,000-500,000 under Art. 229 bis for unlicensed recruitment or outsourcing activity. A skill may multiply the ceiling only by the headcount stated by the user, must label the result `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for the statutory ceiling, and must state that the amount actually charged comes from the HRSD schedule under Art. 230(2), which is `[model knowledge — verify]`.
- **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a row that is missing or tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` (WPS thresholds and fines, the penalties schedule, the Qiwa designation decision, the documentation deadline for legacy contracts, Absher/Muqeem exit rules, wage-clause direct enforcement, Musaned), it must say so, cite the Open questions above, and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must not fill the gap from memory or treat a platform's on-screen behaviour as the legal rule.
# Social Insurance Law — نظام التأمينات الاجتماعية

<!-- Written 2026-09-04 from the Arabic text on laws.boe.gov.sa (new law M/273 of 1445H and the 1421H law M/33 that still governs legacy contributors). The Implementing Regulation (اللائحة التنفيذية) issued by the GOSI Board was NOT in the source set; every rule that the law delegates to the Regulation is flagged. -->

## Instrument

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
| Instrument | Social Insurance Law / نظام التأمينات الاجتماعية (1445H). Read together with the Social Insurance Law 1421H / نظام التأمينات الاجتماعية 1421هـ, which continues to govern contributors with pre-3 July 2024 contribution periods (Royal Decree M/273, clause Fourth) |
| Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/273 of 26/12/1445H (2 July 2024), approving Council of Ministers Decision No. 1022 of 26/12/1445H. The Royal Decree and the Council decision each carry the transitional clauses (Second to Eleventh) that set the contribution phase-in, the retirement-age table, and which law applies to whom. Legacy law: Royal Decree No. M/33 of 3/9/1421H (29 November 2000), Council of Ministers Decision No. 199 of 17/8/1421H |
| Issuing authority | General Organization for Social Insurance (المؤسسة العامة للتأمينات الاجتماعية, GOSI). "The Minister" in the new law is the Minister of Finance (Art. 1). The Implementing Regulation is issued by the GOSI Board of Directors (Art. 62) |
| Effective date | 2024-07-03 (Art. 63: "يعمل به من اليوم التالي لتاريخ صدوره"; issued 2 July 2024; published in Umm al-Qura 29/12/1445H = 5 July 2024). The portal shows issue date 1445/12/26 and publication date 1445/12/29. Application "to new entrants only" is not in the law itself: it follows from Decree clause Second, which excludes (1) contributors with uncompensated contribution periods under the Civil Retirement Law or the 1421H law before 3 July 2024 and (2) existing pensioners; clause Fourth keeps the 1421H law, the Civil Retirement Law (M/41 of 1393H) and the Unemployment Insurance Law (M/18 of 1435H) in force for those two groups. Legacy law: issued 29 November 2000, published 12 January 2001 |
| Amendment history | New law: none; the portal marks 0 articles as amended (`<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 0 -->`) and the status line is "ساري". Legacy 1421H law: portal marks 9 articles as amended (Arts. 2, 10, 37, 38, 41, 43, 58, 58 bis, 62). Of the legacy articles relied on below, Arts. 4, 5, 7, 18, 19, 20 and 65 are unamended; Art. 38 was amended by Royal Decree M/49 of 22/8/1431H (paras 1(c) and 2) and by Royal Decree M/134 of 27/11/1440H (a paragraph deleted); Art. 62 was replaced by Royal Decree M/55 of 10/5/1438H and had para (8) added by Royal Decree M/81 of 19/5/1445H. For those two articles the rows below were read from the "تعديلات المادة" (wording in force) blocks, not the original text |
| Official source (authoritative language) | New law: https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/eaee8a20-3a54-4aaf-b0d9-b1ad00998962/1 . Legacy 1421H law: https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/8ff3cd90-e466-4bf9-a071-a9a700f2a70d/1 |
| Official translation | none. The portal page for the new law carries only the Arabic text and a "notify me when the English version is available" control; no Bureau of Experts translation was in the source set for either law |
| Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 (both laws, Arabic) |
| Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: portal status "ساري" for both laws; 0 amended articles on the new law; the amendment-watch run of 2026-09-04 (`docs/amendment-watch-2026-09-04.md`) surfaced no GOSI or social-insurance lead. Source-copy note: the portal page for the new law carries the full text of Royal Decree M/273 and Council of Ministers Decision 1022 ahead of the law; the first same-day fetch of that page contained the whole decree and decision (clauses First to Eleventh, including the phase-in schedule and retirement-age table) and the rows citing those clauses were read from it; the regenerated scratch copy made later the same day truncates the preamble to the tail of the Council decision (from clause Sixth), so anyone re-verifying from the scratch copy must re-fetch the portal page. Not checked: the GOSI Implementing Regulation and any GOSI Board decision on phasing (Decree clause Third(5) lets the Board phase in the branches); gosi.gov.sa pages are JavaScript-rendered and were not read |

## Scope

The law establishes three social-insurance branches administered by GOSI: the annuities (pensions) branch (فرع المعاشات), the occupational hazards and additional compensations branch (فرع الأخطار المهنية والتعويضات الإضافية), and the unemployment insurance branch (فرع التأمين ضد التعطل عن العمل, marketed as SANED). Every natural or legal person who employs one or more workers or employees for a wage is an "employer" (Art. 1). The annuities and unemployment branches are compulsory for Saudi workers and, for the first time, Saudi public-sector employees (the new law merges the civil retirement and social insurance schemes for new entrants); the occupational hazards branch is compulsory for all workers and employees in the Kingdom regardless of nationality (Art. 28). The law does not apply to anyone who had contribution periods under the 1421H law or the Civil Retirement Law before 3 July 2024, or to existing pensioners: those people remain under the 1421H law (contribution 9% + 9%, retirement at 60) subject to the phased retirement-age and qualifying-period tables in Decree clause Fifth. Practically, every private employer runs two populations side by side: pre-July 2024 hires on the 1421H rates, and later hires on the new law's rising rates. Not covered here: the Implementing Regulation (registration deadlines, wage elements, exemptions from fines), the Civil Retirement Law, and GOSI's optional savings programs (Art. 4).

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Art. 1 (new law) | Definitions. "Employer": every natural or legal person who employs one or more workers or employees for a wage or salary. "Worker": every natural person working for an employer under the employer's management or supervision for a wage. "Contributor": anyone subject to any insurance branch, savings program or insurance product. "Minister": the Minister of Finance. | صاحب العمل: كل شخص طبيعي أو اعتباري يشغّل عاملاً أو موظفاً أو أكثر مقابل أجر أو راتب. العامل: كل شخص طبيعي يعمل لمصلحة صاحب عمل وتحت إدارته أو إشرافه مقابل أجر. الوزير: وزير المالية. | A single employee triggers the employer's registration and contribution duties; there is no headcount threshold. The "management or supervision" test mirrors Labor Law Art. 50, so a worker-classification finding that a contractor is in substance a worker carries a GOSI back-contribution exposure (Art. 9 fine plus Art. 59 penalty). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 3, 4, 5 | The law has three branches: annuities; occupational hazards and additional compensations; unemployment insurance. The Board may create optional savings programs. The Council of Ministers may, on the Board's proposal, create compulsory insurance products for (a) Saudi self-employed and professionals and (b) non-Saudi workers beyond what Art. 6 and the occupational-hazards branch already cover. | يشمل النظام فروع التأمينات الاجتماعية الآتية: فرع المعاشات، فرع الأخطار المهنية والتعويضات الإضافية، فرع التأمين ضد التعطل عن العمل. | Non-Saudi workers today are covered only by the occupational-hazards branch (2% employer-borne, row Art. 28-29); Art. 5(b) is the hook for any future compulsory product for expatriates. A reviewer should not assume any pension contribution for a non-Saudi employee unless a Council decision under Art. 5 is produced. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 6 | Coverage of the following categories is decided by the Board under the Regulation: workers in foreign diplomatic, political or military missions; agricultural, forestry and herding workers; seafarers including fishermen; domestic workers; non-Saudi workers entering on a temporary or seasonal work visa of no more than 60 days; home-workers; players and coaches of sports clubs and federations. | يكون خضوع الفئات المبينة أدناه لأحكام فروع التأمينات الاجتماعية بناءً على ما يقرره مجلس الإدارة ووفقاً للأحكام التي تحددها اللائحة … عمال المنازل … العمال غير السعوديين الذين يأتون إلى المملكة بتأشيرة عمل مؤقتة أو موسمية لمدة لا تزيد على (ستين) يوماً … | These categories are not excluded outright (unlike the 1421H law Art. 5, which excluded them): they are covered if and when the Board says so. For a corporate employer the relevant ones are short-visit (≤60-day visa) assignees and, for sports entities, players and coaches. Whether the Board has switched coverage on for any category was not verifiable from the law text. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 7 | The Regulation sets: how employers and contributors register; a grace period for employers to register contributors who meet the conditions and to de-register those who no longer do; a grace period for a contributor to register or de-register himself if the employer fails or delays; and the rules for registration after the grace periods lapse. | تحدد اللائحة … مهلة لأصحاب العمل للاشتراك عن المشتركين … ومهلة للمشترك لطلب إشراك نفسه … إذا امتنع صاحب العمل أو تراخى عن الاشتراك أو الاستبعاد. | The registration deadline is in the Regulation, not the law; the GOSI practice deadline is 15 days from joining and 15 days from leaving, applied through the GOSI online portal `[model knowledge — verify]`. Under Art. 31(2) an injury before registration and after the grace period is uncompensated by GOSI, which pushes the cost onto the employer. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 8 | Contributions for every branch are calculated on the basic wage or salary; the Regulation may add other elements and allowances the contributor receives, provided the base is not below the minimum wage set by the competent authority. The wage ceiling subject to contribution is SAR 45,000 per month, adjustable by Council of Ministers decision on the Board's proposal after actuarial review. The employer must submit wage data to GOSI in the manner and within the period the Regulation sets. | تحسب الاشتراكات لكل فرع … على أساس الأجر أو الراتب الأساس، وللائحة إضافة أي عناصر وبدلات أخرى … يكون الحد الأعلى للأجر أو الراتب الخاضع للاشتراك (45,000) خمسة وأربعين ألف ريال شهريًّا. يجب على صاحب العمل أن يقدم إلى المؤسسة … بيانات بالأجور أو الرواتب الخاضعة للاشتراك. | The law fixes only "basic wage" plus whatever the Regulation adds; the Regulation has historically added the housing allowance (or in-kind housing valued at two months' basic wage per year) and commissions, and the same definition carries over `[model knowledge — verify]`. When reviewing an offer letter, split basic wage and housing from other allowances: the first two drive GOSI cost, the rest generally do not. Wage under-declaration to GOSI (declaring basic only, paying more) is a common diligence finding and is penalised under Art. 59. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 9 | The employer must pay the full contributions due from itself and from the contributor within the period the Regulation sets and is solely liable to GOSI for payment; it may deduct the contributor's share from the wage each time the wage is paid. Late payment attracts a fine of 2% of the contribution due for each month or part of a month of delay; the Regulation sets the cases of full or partial exemption. Only periods for which contributions are paid count towards benefits; counting is suspended when unpaid periods reach the length set by the Board (not less than 6 months), unless an event prevents the establishment from paying. | يلتزم صاحب العمل بدفع كامل مبالغ الاشتراكات المستحقة عنه وعن المشترك … وهو وحده المسؤول تجاه المؤسسة عن دفعها … تفرض عليه غرامة قدرها (2 %) من الاشتراك المستحق عن كل شهر تأخير أو جزء منه. | The 2% per month is a delay fine, not interest, and is not capped in the law (the 1421H law Art. 19(5) left the cap to the Regulation). The employee's share, once deducted, is held for GOSI; failing to remit is the employer's liability alone. The payment deadline is in the Regulation; under the 1421H law it was the first 15 days of the following month (row on legacy Art. 19) and GOSI practice keeps that deadline `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 10 | Government bodies, public institutions and authorities, state-owned companies and companies in which the State holds more than 50% of the capital must require any employer dealing with them to produce a GOSI certificate showing the establishment is registered and has met all its obligations (or is not subject to the law) when the employer: (a) applies to collect its dues; (b) bids for public works, supply, operation, maintenance or other projects; (c) applies to amend, renew or cancel its commercial register; (d) applies for a State subsidy; (e) seeks liquidation of its establishment; (f) applies for or renews a project licence; (g) applies to recruit workers from abroad. | على الجهات الحكومية … مطالبةُ صاحب العمل الذي يتعامل معها بأن يقدم شهادة صادرة من المؤسسة يثبت فيها أن منشأته مسجلة لديها، وأنه قد قام بجميع التزاماته تجاهها … التقدم بطلب تعديل سجله التجاري أو تجديده أو شطبه … التقدم بطلب استقدام عمال من الخارج. | This is the statutory basis for "GOSI arrears block services". In diligence, a target with GOSI arrears cannot cleanly renew or amend its commercial register, obtain new recruitment visas, collect government receivables, or be liquidated until the arrears are settled; the block is implemented electronically through the Ministry of Commerce, Qiwa and government-payment integrations rather than by a paper certificate `[model knowledge — verify]`. Ask for the GOSI compliance certificate (شهادة الالتزام) and the GOSI statement of account as standard closing deliverables. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 11 | Contributions and late-payment fines are secured by a lien in GOSI's favour ranking immediately after the lien for wages. GOSI may, through the competent bodies and by the applicable judicial and statutory procedures, attach and enforce against any assets owned by or owed to the employer, once the schedules of amounts due are officially certified by the Minister or his delegate. | مبالغ الاشتراكات والغرامات … مضمونة بحق امتياز لصالح المؤسسة، تأتي مباشرة في الدرجة التي تلي امتياز استيفاء الأجور. للمؤسسة … إجراء الحجز والتنفيذ الجبري … على أي أموال مملوكة لصاحب العمل أو مستحقة له. | GOSI ranks second only to wage claims in an insolvency or enforcement waterfall (cross-check the Bankruptcy Law priority rules before relying on this in a distressed-target memo). Certified GOSI schedules are enforceable through the Enforcement Court without a prior judgment on the merits; the employer's remedy is objection under Art. 60(3). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 14 | The annuities branch applies compulsorily to (a) all Saudi employees (public sector) and (b) all Saudi workers working inside the Kingdom, or outside it for an employer whose head office is in the Kingdom, provided the worker or employee is below the statutory entitlement age when the law first applies to him. It applies optionally to Saudis working abroad with no Kingdom-based employer. | يطبق فرع المعاشات بصورة إلزامية على … جميع العمال السعوديين الذين يعملون داخل المملكة، أو خارجها لحساب صاحب عمل مقره الرئيس داخل المملكة. | A Saudi national seconded abroad by a Saudi-headquartered employer stays in the annuities branch. A Saudi hired locally by a foreign affiliate with no Saudi head office is outside compulsory coverage (may opt in). A Saudi hired at or after the statutory age is outside the branch. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 15 with Royal Decree M/273 clause Third(1)-(3) | Annuities contribution: 22% of the contributory wage, 11% employer and 11% contributor (optional contributors bear the whole 22%). The Decree phases the rate in for those covered by the new law: 18% during the 12 months from the effective date; 19% from the month following the lapse of 12 months; 20% from the month following 24 months; 21% from the month following 36 months; 22% from the month following 48 months; the employer bears 50% and the contributor 50% of the compulsory contribution at each step. The Council of Ministers may amend the rates on the Board's proposal after an actuarial study. | يكون الاشتراك في فرع المعاشات بنسبة (22 %) … يتحمل صاحب العمل (11 %)، ويتحمل المشترك (11 %). المرسوم، ثالثاً: تطبق نسب الاشتراكات لفرع المعاشات … تدريجيًّا حتى بلوغها نسبة (22%) … أ- خلال الاثني عشر شهراً من تاريخ نفاذ النظام بنسبة (18%) … هـ- من الشهر التالي لانقضاء ثمانية وأربعين شهراً … بنسبة (22%). يتحمل صاحب العمل (50%) ويتحمل المشترك (50%). | For employees who joined the scheme on or after 3 July 2024: 9% + 9% (from 3 July 2024), 9.5% + 9.5% (from mid-2025), 10% + 10% (from mid-2026), 10.5% + 10.5% (from mid-2027), 11% + 11% (from mid-2028). The exact switch-over month is ambiguous in the text: "the month following the lapse of twelve months from the effective date" reads as August 2025 on a strict count from 3 July 2024, while GOSI has applied each step from July `[model knowledge — verify]`. Employees with pre-July 2024 periods stay on the 1421H rate of 9% + 9% (row on legacy Art. 18); the Decree's phase-in applies only to "those covered by the new law". A hiring-review cost model therefore needs the hire date and prior-GOSI status of each Saudi employee, not just the salary. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 16 with Council of Ministers Decision 1022 clause Third(5) | The statutory entitlement age is 65. A contributor is entitled to a pension on reaching 65 with at least the minimum contribution period set by Council of Ministers decision; Decision 1022 sets that minimum at 180 months. Early pension: up to 120 months before 65 if the contributor has stopped work subject to the law and has at least 360 months of contributions (subject to the Art. 17(3) reduction). | يقصد بالسن النظامية للاستحقاق … سن (الخامسة والستين). … يحق للمشترك الحصول على المعاش قبل بلوغه السن النظامية للاستحقاق بما لا يزيد على (120) شهراً … وكانت مدة اشتراكه (360) شهراً على الأقل. قرار مجلس الوزراء: تكون مدة الاشتراك المشار إليها في الفقرة (2) من المادة (السادسة عشرة) من النظام (180) شهراً. | Retirement age for new entrants is 65 (both sexes), against 60 under the 1421H law. Labor Law Art. 74(4), as amended by Royal Decree M/134 of 27/11/1440H, now ends the contract on "the worker reaching retirement age as provided in the Social Insurance Law, unless the parties agree to continue" (the original 60 men / 55 women wording is no longer in force), so the Labor Law retirement trigger follows this row: 65 for new-law contributors, 60 for legacy contributors, or the Decree clause Fifth phased age in between. Termination-review must first classify the employee (new-law, legacy, or phased) before treating "retirement age" as a lawful end of contract. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 17, 26 | Pension = 2.25% × average contributory wage (average of the highest 180 months of contributory wages, Art. 26) × months of contribution ÷ 12, capped at 100% of that average; the Board may add benefits above the cap. Minimum pension SAR 4,000 per month at 480 months, reduced proportionately for each 12 months (and each month) short of 480, with a floor of SAR 2,000 at the 180-month qualifying period. Early pension is reduced by 3% for each 12 months (pro rata per month) before 65 or short of 480 months, whichever is less, for the life of the pension; a pension claimed after continuing work beyond 65 is increased by 3% per 12 months. | يُحتسب المعاش … بضرب (2.25 %) في متوسط الأجر أو الراتب الخاضع للاشتراك … في عدد أشهر الاشتراك وتقسيم الناتج على (12)، على ألا يتجاوز المعاش (100 %) من ذلك المتوسط … يكون الحد الأدنى للمعاش المستحق للمشترك الذي بلغت مدة اشتراكه (480) شهراً (4000) ريال شهرياً … يتم إنقاص المعاش … بنسبة (3 %) عن كل (12) شهراً. | Compared with the 1421H law (2.5% accrual on the last 24 months' average, Art. 38 legacy), the new formula accrues more slowly and on a longer averaging window. Relevant to termination-review only as context for early-retirement negotiations; the numbers are GOSI's to compute, and a skill must not compute an individual's pension from this row. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 25, 27 | A contributor who reaches 65 (or is disabled under Art. 21) and stops covered work without qualifying for a pension receives: with 60 months or more of contributions, a compensation based on the total employer and contributor contributions revalued (not less than the total paid), payable monthly or as a lump sum at his option; with less than 60 months, a lump sum on the same basis. On death without pension entitlement the family receives the full lump sum. A contributor who loses Saudi nationality receives a lump sum. | إذا كانت مدة الاشتراك (60) شهراً فأكثر، يحق للمشترك الحصول على تعويض محسوب على أساس مجموع الاشتراكات المؤداة من صاحب العمل والمشترك … بعد إعادة تثمينها … إذا لم يعد المشترك متمتعاً بالجنسية العربية السعودية، فإنه يمنح تعويض دفعة واحدة. | The employer's contributions are not refundable to the employer in any scenario; they follow the contributor. No employer-side action is needed at termination beyond de-registration (Art. 7). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 28, 29 with Decree clause Third(4) | The occupational hazards and additional compensations branch applies compulsorily to all employees and all workers working inside the Kingdom (no nationality condition). Contribution: 2% of the contributory wage, borne by the employer; the Board may, for a fixed period, raise it up to double for employers who refuse to comply with instructions on contributors' safety and health; the Council of Ministers may amend the rate after an actuarial study. The date on which the branch applies to public employees is set by Council of Ministers decision. | يطبق فرع الأخطار المهنية والتعويضات الإضافية بصورة إلزامية على … جميع العمال الذين يعملون داخل المملكة. تحدد اشتراكات فرع الأخطار المهنية … بـ (2 %) من الأجر أو الراتب الخاضع للاشتراك، يتحملها صاحب العمل، ويجوز … زيادة هذه النسبة بما لا يتجاوز الضعف بالنسبة لأصحاب العمل الذين يرفضون التقيد بالتعليمات الصادرة في موضوع سلامة المشتركين وصحتهم. | Every non-Saudi employee costs the employer 2% of contributory wage (capped at SAR 45,000 base) and nothing else at GOSI. Every Saudi employee costs 2% plus the annuities and unemployment shares. The doubling power is the only GOSI-side sanction tied to OSH non-compliance; the main one is Labor Law Art. 229 as amended by Royal Decree M/46 of 1436H (fine up to SAR 100,000, closure up to 30 days or permanently, per-person multiplication). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 30, 31 | "Work injury" is any accident occurring during or because of work, including on the usual route between home and work, between work and the usual place of meals or prayer, and on travel for an assignment given by the employer; occupational diseases listed in the Board's schedule count as work injuries, the date of first medical observation being the injury date. Benefits: medical care; daily allowances for temporary disability; monthly annuities and lump-sum compensation for permanent total or partial disability. Benefits are due only if the injury occurred after the contributor's registration with GOSI was completed, or within the employer's registration grace period, or within the contributor's self-registration period where the employer failed to register him. | تعد إصابة عمل كل حادث يقع للمشترك أثناء العمل أو بسببه، ويعد في حكم ذلك كل حادث يقع للمشترك أثناء طريقه المعتاد من مسكنه إلى مقر عمله والعكس … لا تستحق التعويضات … إلا إذا كانت الإصابة قد وقعت بعد إتمام إجراءات تسجيل المشترك لدى المؤسسة، أو خلال المهلة التي تحددها اللائحة لصاحب العمل لتسجيل مشتركيه. | Commuting accidents are covered. The registration condition in Art. 31(2) is the sharpest exposure for an employer that registers late: an injury to an unregistered worker after the grace period is not compensated by GOSI and the worker will pursue the employer under Labor Law Arts. 132-140 and general liability `[model knowledge — verify]`. Art. 32(3) separately obliges the employer to provide first aid. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 33 | Temporary disability from a work injury reported within the Regulation's deadlines: a daily allowance for every day off work including holidays, from the day after the injury until recovery, confirmation of permanent disability, or death. The allowance is 100% of the daily contributory wage of the month before the injury (or the joining month), reduced to 75% while the injured is treated at GOSI's expense as an in-patient. | للمصاب في حالة عجزه المؤقت عن العمل … الحق في بدل يومي عن كل يوم إقعاد عن العمل بما في ذلك أيام العطل … يقدر البدل اليومي للعجز المؤقت بواقع (100 %) من أجر أو راتب الاشتراك اليومي … ويخفض إلى (75 %) … خلال فترة تلقيه العلاج على نفقة المؤسسة في أحد المستشفيات. | GOSI, not the employer, pays wages during work-injury absence once the injury is reported in time; the employer should not run injury absence through sick leave under Labor Law Art. 117. Late reporting (deadlines in the Regulation) shifts the cost back to the employer `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 35, 36, 39 | Permanent total disability: a monthly annuity of 100% of the average contributory wage of the three months before the injury (not below the Art. 17 minimum). Permanent partial disability of 50% or more: the total-disability annuity multiplied by the disability percentage. Below 50%: a lump sum of 60 times the corresponding monthly annuity (not less than 36 times), reduced by one monthly annuity for each year of age above 45 at the injury date. Annuities are provisional for five years with periodic re-examination, increased 50% where constant attendance is needed, and may be combined with wages. A non-Saudi entitled to an annuity (or his family on death) receives a lump sum instead: 84 times the monthly annuity for total disability, 60 times for partial, 84 times on death. | في حالة العجز الكلي المستديم … عائدة شهرية تعادل (100 %) من متوسط الأجر … يدفع إلى المصاب غير السعودي المستحق لعائدة شهرية أو لأفراد عائلته في حال الوفاة تعويض مقطوع بدلاً من العائدة … (84) مرة من العائدة الشهرية المحددة للعجز الكلي المستديم. | The non-Saudi lump-sum substitution (Art. 39) is the rule an HR adviser most often needs when an expatriate is injured: the family of a deceased expatriate receives 84 months' annuity as a lump sum from GOSI, on top of the employer's Labor Law Art. 40(4) repatriation duty (which the employer is excused from where GOSI pays under Art. 40 of this law). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 41, 42 with Decree clause Sixth(1) | Maternity compensation: a female contributor who gives birth receives compensation for 3 months (plus one month where the child is sick or disabled and needs constant care), if she has at least 12 months of contributions, continuous or not, in the 36 months before the birth and is in covered employment at the birth, and the birth is at least six months into the pregnancy. The compensation is 100% of the average contributory wage of the 12 months before the birth, payable from the first day of the birth month (claimable one month before the expected date), and may not be combined with wages. The Decree extends maternity compensation to contributors under the 1421H law's occupational-hazards branch, counting the qualifying period from 3 July 2024. | يحق للمشتركة في حال الولادة الحصول على تعويض الأمومة لمدة (3) أشهر … أن يكون لديها مدة اشتراك لا تقل عن (12) شهراً … يحسب تعويض الأمومة بواقع (100 %) من متوسط الأجر … لا يجوز الجمع بين تعويض الأمومة والأجر أو الراتب. | GOSI now pays three months of maternity compensation for qualifying Saudi employees (and, through the Decree, for 1421H-law contributors in the occupational-hazards branch, which includes non-Saudi women). How this interacts with the employer's paid maternity leave under Labor Law Art. 151 (12 weeks fully paid after the 1446H amendment `[model knowledge — verify]`) is a live question: the law bars combining the compensation with wages, which suggests the employer's wage obligation is displaced for the covered months, but GOSI's implementing rules were not available. Do not advise on the split without the Regulation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 43, 44 with Decision 1022 clause Third(6) | Unemployment insurance (SANED) applies compulsorily to all Saudi workers in the Kingdom who are under 64 when the law first applies to them; contributions stop when the contributor reaches the pension entitlement age. Contribution in the law: 2% of the contributory wage, 1% employer and 1% contributor; the Council of Ministers may vary it between 0.5% and 2% split equally. Decision 1022 sets the rate at the effective date at 1.5%, 0.75% employer and 0.75% contributor. | يطبق فرع التأمين ضد التعطل عن العمل بصورة إلزامية على جميع العمال السعوديين الذين يعملون داخل المملكة … تحدد اشتراكات فرع التأمين ضد التعطل عن العمل بنسبة (2 %) … (1 %) يتحملها صاحب العمل، و(1 %) يتحملها المشترك. قرار مجلس الوزراء: تحدد الاشتراكات في فرع التأمين ضد التعطل عن العمل في تاريخ نفاذ النظام بنسبة (1,5%) … بواقع (50%) لكل منهما. | The operative SANED rate is 0.75% + 0.75%, set by the Council decision, not the 1% + 1% printed in Art. 44. Total employer GOSI cost for a new-law Saudi employee in 2026: annuities 10% (mid-2026 step) + occupational hazards 2% + SANED 0.75% = 12.75% of contributory wage; employee share 10% + 0.75% = 10.75% `[computed from the rows above; the annuities step date is the ambiguity noted at Art. 15]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 45-49 | SANED benefit conditions: Saudi nationality; qualifying period met; not dismissed for a reason attributable to the worker; no private business or work income; did not leave voluntarily; able to work; below pension age; registered within the Regulation's deadline after leaving; actively seeking work; complying with training. Qualifying periods: 12 months in the 36 before the first claim, 18 for the second, 24 for the third, 36 in 48 thereafter. Benefit: up to 12 months per entitlement within 24 months; 60% of the average monthly wage (1/24 of the last 24 months' contributory wages) for the first three months and 50% thereafter, capped at SAR 9,000 then SAR 7,500 per month. Payment stops on death, pension entitlement, or loss of any condition. | ألا يكون مفصولاً من العمل بسبب راجع إليه … ألا يكون تركه العمل بسبب عدم رغبته في الاستمرار فيه … يصرف تعويض فرع التأمين ضد التعطل عن العمل بواقع (60 %) من متوسط الأجور الشهرية عن كل شهر من الأشهر الثلاثة الأولى، وبواقع (50 %) … الحد الأعلى لمبلغ التعويض (٩٫٠٠٠) … و(٧٫٥٠٠). | Termination-review point: the reason code the employer records when de-registering the employee at GOSI (resignation, Art. 77 termination, Art. 80 dismissal) determines SANED eligibility; a dismissal recorded under Labor Law Art. 80 disqualifies the worker, so employees contest the coding `[model knowledge — verify]`. Mutual-separation agreements should state the exit category and who submits the GOSI notification. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 54, 57 | Claims for daily injury allowances and travel/accommodation allowances lapse one year after they fall due; claims for other benefits lapse after five years, unless GOSI accepts an excuse. Cash benefits may be attached only by court judgment, up to 25% for one creditor and 50% for several, maintenance debts ranking first; GOSI itself has a priority right to set off its own claims up to 25% (more where the benefit is a lump sum or the debt arose from incorrect data). | لا تقبل المطالبة بالبدلات اليومية للإصابة … التي مضى على استحقاقها سنة واحدة، ولا تقبل المطالبة بالتعويضات الأخرى التي مضى على استحقاقها خمس سنوات … لا يجوز الحجز على التعويضات النقدية … إلا بحكم قضائي على ألا تتجاوز نسبة الحسم للدائن الواحد (25 %). | An employer creditor (loan, training bond) cannot reach GOSI benefits except through a judgment and within the 25% cap. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 58 | GOSI inspectors appointed by the Governor monitor compliance; employers must give inspectors the information and documents needed, including records on the nature of work, the numbers of workers and employees, their wages and salaries, and how they are calculated and paid. Inspectors report violations; GOSI investigates. Inspectors and staff are bound to confidentiality, with exceptions for enforcement, consent, or judicial and public-authority requests. A financial reward may be given to anyone, inspector or not, who helps uncover a violation. | على أصحاب العمل أن يسمحوا للمفتشين بالحصول على المعلومات والوثائق اللازِمة … بما في ذلك الاطلاع على السجِلات المتعلقة بطبيعة العمل، وأعداد العمال والموظفين وأجورهم ورواتبهم، وطريقة حسابها ودفعها … يجوز منح مكافأة مالية لمن يساعد … في الكشف عن أي من مخالفات أحكام النظام واللائحة. | Payroll records must reconcile to GOSI wage declarations; the whistle-blower reward makes under-declaration a reportable risk from inside the workforce. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 59, 60, 61 | Penalties, without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law: a fine up to SAR 50,000 for any violation of the law or Regulation, doubled for a repeat within three years, and multiplied by the number of contributors concerned. Registering a person who does not actually work for the employer: the same fine or up to twice the total contributions for the period, whichever is higher, per person, with the contributions refunded to the employer. Where a violation causes benefits to be paid without right: the fine or the amount of the benefits, whichever is higher, plus restitution. No fine after five years from the violation (correction and restitution still due). The Board issues a schedule classifying violations; GOSI may settle and waive fines. A three-member committee (at least one legal specialist) imposes penalties; appeal lies to the competent court. Publication of the decision at the violator's expense may be ordered once final. | يعاقب كل من يخالف أي حكم من أحكام النظام واللائحة بغرامة لا تزيد على (50.000) خمسين ألف ريال، وتضاعف الغرامة المفروضة في حال تكرار المخالفة … وتتعدد الغرامة بتعدد المشتركين الذين ارتكب صاحب العمل بصددهم المخالفة. يعاقب صاحب العمل الذي يسجل شخصاً يثبت لدى المؤسسة أنه لا يعمل لمصلحته … | The per-contributor multiplier makes the ceiling effectively unlimited for systemic non-registration (10 unregistered workers = up to SAR 500,000). The fictitious-registration offence targets "ghost Saudis" registered to inflate the Nitaqat Saudization count; it is the GOSI-side complement to the HRSD penalties for fake Saudization and is a standard diligence red flag when Nitaqat headcount and payroll do not match. The five-year limitation runs from the violation, so a diligence look-back of five years of GOSI filings is the right scope. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Royal Decree M/273 clauses Second, Fourth, Fifth | The new law does not apply to (1) contributors with contribution periods before 3 July 2024 under the Civil Retirement Law or the 1421H law that have not been compensated, or (2) pensioners under those laws; for them the 1421H law, the Civil Retirement Law and the Unemployment Insurance Law of 1435H remain in force. By exception, contributors with fewer than 240 months of such periods who were under 50 Hijri years (48 years 6 months Gregorian) on 3 July 2024 get a phased pension age: under 29 → 65; 29 → 64y 8m; 30 → 64y 4m; 31 → 64; 32 → 63y 8m; 33 → 63y 4m; 34 → 63; 35 → 62y 8m; 36 → 62y 4m; 37 → 62; 38 → 61y 8m; 39 → 61y 4m; 40 → 61; 41 → 60y 8m; 42 → 60y 4m; 43 → 60; 44 → 59y 8m; 45 → 59y 4m; 46 → 59; 47 → 58y 8m; 48 to under 48y 6m → 58y 4m (age bands are "X years or more and under X+1"). Early-pension qualifying period for that group: fewer than 180 months at 3 July 2024 → 360 months; 180-191 → 348; 192-203 → 336; 204-215 → 324; 216-227 → 312; 228-239 → 300. | ثانياً: لا تسري أحكام النظام … على الفئتين الآتيتين: المشتركون الذين لديهم مدد اشتراك سابقة على نفاذ النظام لم يعوضوا عنها … أصحاب المعاشات … رابعاً: يستمر العمل بالأحكام الواردة في نظام التأمينات الاجتماعية الصادر بالمرسوم الملكي رقم (م / 33) … خامساً: … المشترك الذي لم يبلغ في تاريخ نفاذ النظام سن (التاسعة والعشرين) ميلادية، تكون السن النظامية لاستحقاقه للمعاش سن (الخامسة والستين) ميلادية. | The "who is on which law" test is: any GOSI or civil-retirement contribution month before 3 July 2024 that was not paid out as a lump sum keeps the employee on the 1421H law (rates, formula, branches), but with the new retirement age from this table if the employee was under 48.5 and had under 240 months on that date. An employee aged 48.5 or more, or with 240+ months, keeps age 60. A workforce census by hire date, age on 3 July 2024 and prior contribution months is the only way to cost retirement timing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| 1421H law Art. 4, 5, 7 (legacy contributors) | Occupational-hazards branch applies compulsorily to all workers without distinction of sex, nationality or age; annuities branch compulsorily to all Saudi workers under 60 when first covered (special rules for returners aged 60-65). Condition: work under a contract of employment for one or more employers, whatever the contract's term, nature, form or wage, performed mainly inside the Kingdom, or a Saudi working abroad for a Kingdom-headquartered employer. Excluded: civil servants and military under their own schemes, foreign staff of foreign missions, agricultural and herding workers, seafarers, domestic servants, foreign workers on jobs normally under three months, home-workers, family members in family-only businesses (with opt-in for the last two). Employers must apply the law from the day the conditions are met; the Regulation sets registration. | يُطبَّق فرع الأخطار المِهنية بصورة إلزامية على جميع العُمال دون أي تمييز في الجنس أو الجنسية أو السِّن. يُطبَّق فرع المعاشات بصورة إلزامية على جميع العُمال السعوديين … بشرط أن يكون سِن العامِل عند بدء تطبيق النظام عليه دون سِن الستين … يُستثنى … خَدم المنازِل … العُمال الأجانِب الذين يقدمون إلى المملكة لأعمال لا يستغرق إنجازُها في المُعتاد أكثر من ثلاثة أشهُر. | For pre-July 2024 hires the exclusion list is closed (domestic workers, sub-three-month foreign assignments, etc.). For a short-term foreign assignee under the legacy regime the three-month "normally" test applies; under the new law the test is a visa of 60 days or less subject to a Board decision (Art. 6). Because new hires are on the new law, the legacy exclusions matter only for the existing population. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| 1421H law Art. 18, 19, 65 (legacy contributors) | Occupational hazards: 2% of the contributory wage, employer-borne, may be doubled for employers refusing OSH instructions. Annuities: 18% of the contributory wage, 9% employer and 9% worker (Art. 65 phased the worker's share 6% → 7.5% → 9% over the first two years after 2001). Contributions are calculated on the monthly basic wage as defined by the Regulation, which may add allowances and in-kind benefits; ceiling SAR 45,000 per month; wage increases after age 50 count only within the Regulation's limits. The employer pays both shares and is solely liable; it may deduct the worker's share at each wage payment, and if it fails to deduct at the time it may not recover later. Contributions are due within the first 15 days of the following month; late payment carries a 2% fine per month or part, with the cap and exemptions in the Regulation. The employer must file monthly wage schedules within the same deadline. State bodies and companies in which joint-stock companies hold shares must demand a GOSI certificate for dues collection, tenders, commercial-register changes, subsidies, liquidation, recruitment approvals and licensing. | تُحدَّد اشتِراكات فرع الأخطار المِهنية بـ(2%) … ويقع دفعُها على عاتق صاحِب العمل … يُحدَّد اشتِراك فرع المعاشات … بنِسبة (18%) … يدفع صاحِب العمل (9%) … و (9%) يدفعُها المُشترِك … يكون الحد الأعلى للأجر الخاضِع للاشتِراك (45.000) … يجب دفع الاشتِراكات إلى المؤسسة خِلال الخمسة عشر يوماً الأولى من الشهر الذي يلي الشهر المُستحقة عنه … وإذا أهمل صاحِب العمل اقتِطاع هذا الاشتِراك حين دفع الأجور، فليس له أنَّ يقتطِعُه فيما بعد. | Legacy population cost: employer 9% + 2% = 11% for Saudis (plus SANED 0.75% under the 1435H Unemployment Insurance Law, which remains in force for this group by Decree clause Fourth; the 0.75% figure is from the SANED regime as amended and was not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`), 2% for non-Saudis. The "no later deduction" rule in Art. 19(4) means an employer that forgot to withhold the worker's 9% cannot claw it back from later wages; it is an employer loss. The 15-day deadline and monthly wage schedule are the operational rhythm GOSI still runs `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| 1421H law Art. 20, 38 (as amended by M/49 of 1431H and M/134 of 1440H), 62 (as replaced by M/55 of 1438H and M/81 of 1445H) (legacy contributors) | Contributions and delay fines are secured by a lien ranking after wages; GOSI may attach the employer's assets after a court order once schedules are certified by the Minister. Retirement pension at 60 with at least 120 months of contributions; early pension before 60 with 300 months for a contributor no longer covered; sentence of imprisonment with 120 months lets the family draw it; a contributor at 60 with at least five years may buy up to five notional years (para 2 as reworded by M/49). The portal records that M/49 of 1431H reworded para 1(c) (a female contributor no longer covered may draw her pension at 55 with 120 months) and that M/134 of 1440H then deleted a paragraph of the article; read with M/134's simultaneous amendment of Labor Law Art. 74(4), the deleted paragraph is the women-at-55 provision, so the legacy age is 60 for both sexes `[model knowledge — verify]` for the identification of the deleted paragraph, which the portal note does not name. Pension formula: 1/40 of the average wage of the last 24 months per year of contribution, capped at 100% of that average, the average not to exceed 150% of the wage at the start of the last five years. Penalties (Art. 62 in force): a fine up to SAR 10,000 for any violation of the law or regulations, including submitting incorrect data or withholding data GOSI requests, doubled on repetition, multiplied by the number of contributors concerned and by the number of data items submitted or withheld; registering a person who does not work for the employer: that fine or up to twice the contributions for the period, whichever is higher, per person; wrongful benefits recovered plus a fine up to their amount; office committees investigate and refer to the Governor, who issues the decision, with objection within 30 days under Art. 61; publication of the decision may be ordered; no fine after five years; GOSI may settle and waive fines (para 8, added by M/81 of 19/5/1445H). | إذا بلغ المشترك ستين سنة كاملة، وتوقف عن ممارسة أي نشاط خاضع للنظام، يحق له الحصول على معاش التقاعد متى كانت مدة اشتراكه (120) مائة وعشرين شهراً على الأقل … يعاقب كل من يخالف أي حكم من أحكام هذا النظام ولوائحه – بما في ذلك تقديم أي بيانات غير صحيحة أو الامتناع عن تقديم البيانات التي تطلبها المؤسسة – بغرامة لا تزيد على (10000) عشرة آلاف ريال، ويضاعف هذا الحد في حالة التكرار، وتتعدد الغرامة بتعدد العمال المشتركين … كما تتعدد بتعدد البيانات المقدمة أو الممتنع عن تقديمها … يجوز للمؤسسة التسوية مع المخالف للإعفاء من كل أو بعض الغرامات. | The original Art. 62 (SAR 5,000 per violation with a SAR 50,000 aggregate cap) is not in force; since M/55 of 1438H the legacy ceiling is SAR 10,000 per violation with per-contributor and per-data-item multipliers and no aggregate cap, and the ghost-registration offence and the five-year limitation already existed under the legacy law. The new law's Art. 59 raises the per-violation ceiling to SAR 50,000. Which regime applies to a violation concerning a legacy contributor after 3 July 2024 is not settled by the text (Decree clause Fourth keeps the 1421H "provisions" in force for those contributors); assume the new law's Art. 59 for anything GOSI enforces today and flag the point `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- **Exact step dates for the annuities phase-in.** Decree clause Third(1) fixes 18% "during the twelve months from the effective date" and 19% "from the month following the lapse of twelve months". Counting from 3 July 2024, the twelve months lapse on 2 July 2025 and the "following month" is August 2025; GOSI's public communications applied the 9.5% + 9.5% step from July 2025. Confirm which month GOSI billed and whether the 2026 step (10% + 10%) was applied from July or August 2026.
- **Which penalty regime applies to legacy-contributor violations after 3 July 2024** (new Art. 59, SAR 50,000 per violation with per-contributor multiplier, or 1421H Art. 62 as amended by M/55 of 1438H, SAR 10,000 per violation with per-contributor and per-data-item multipliers). Decree clause Fourth continues the 1421H "provisions" for legacy contributors without carving out enforcement.
- **Which paragraph of 1421H Art. 38 was deleted by M/134 of 1440H.** The portal note records the deletion without naming the paragraph; the row above reads it as para 1(c) (women's pension at 55), consistent with M/134's amendment of Labor Law Art. 74(4). Confirm.
- **Interaction of GOSI maternity compensation (Arts. 41-42) with the employer's paid maternity leave under Labor Law Art. 151 as amended by M/44 of 1446H.** The law bars combining compensation with wages; whether the employer's wage obligation is displaced, topped up, or reimbursed is a Regulation and GOSI-practice question. Council of Ministers Decision 1022 clause Eleventh directed an HRSD-led committee to review, within 90 days, the maternity and childbirth leave provisions of the Labor Law and other laws' retirement-age provisions for alignment with the new law; whether that review produced an instrument was not verifiable from the sources.
- **Contributory wage elements under the new Implementing Regulation.** Art. 8 fixes only "basic wage" and delegates the rest; confirm whether housing allowance (and the two-months-per-year valuation of in-kind housing) and commissions carry over from the 1421H Regulation.
- **Registration and payment deadlines** (Art. 7, Art. 9): the 15-day figures used in practice come from the 1421H law and GOSI practice; confirm the new Regulation's periods and the Board's threshold for suspending period-counting (not less than six months, Art. 9(4)).
- **Board decisions under Art. 6** switching coverage on for domestic workers, sub-60-day visa holders, and sports players and coaches; and any Council decision under Art. 5 creating compulsory products for non-Saudi workers.
- **Whether GOSI's per-contributor "ghost registration" fine (Art. 59(2)) is being applied cumulatively with HRSD's fake-Saudization penalties** for the same facts, and GOSI's settlement practice under Art. 59(6).
- **SANED rate for legacy contributors.** The 0.75% + 0.75% used above for the legacy group rests on the Unemployment Insurance Law of 1435H as amended, which was not in the source set; Decision 1022 fixes 1.5% only for new-law contributors.

## How skills should use this file

- `hiring-review` (employment-legal), at the compensation-and-benefits step: cite Art. 1 (employer definition), Art. 8 (contributory wage and SAR 45,000 ceiling), Art. 15 with Decree clause Third (annuities phase-in), Art. 28-29 (2% occupational hazards for all nationalities) and Art. 43-44 with Decision 1022 (SANED 0.75% + 0.75%) to state the employer's GOSI cost for a proposed hire. The skill must ask for the candidate's nationality, hire date and whether the candidate has any pre-3 July 2024 GOSI or civil-retirement contribution months; if unknown, it must present both the new-law and the 1421H-law cost and say which fact decides.
- `termination-review` (employment-legal), at the separation-terms step: cite Art. 16 with Decision 1022 (age 65, 180 months) and Decree clause Fifth (phased age table) alongside Labor Law Art. 74(4); cite Art. 45 (SANED disqualification for dismissal attributable to the worker) when the exit reason is being negotiated. The SANED coding practice point is `[model knowledge — verify]` and must be flagged as such.
- `worker-classification` (employment-legal): cite Art. 1 ("worker" = under the employer's management or supervision for a wage) and Art. 59 (fines per contributor, five-year limitation) when quantifying misclassification exposure; do not compute back-contributions without the contributory-wage rows and the hire-date test.
- `entity-compliance` (corporate-legal), in the periodic-obligations checklist: cite Art. 7 (registration), Art. 8(4) and legacy Art. 19(8) (wage data filing), Art. 9 (monthly payment, 2% delay fine), Art. 10 (certificate for commercial-register renewal, licensing, recruitment), Art. 58 (inspection access).
- `diligence-issue-extraction` and `tabular-review` (corporate-legal): for each target entity, extract the GOSI registration number, the GOSI compliance certificate, the statement of account, and a headcount reconciliation between GOSI, Qiwa and payroll; cite Art. 10 (services block), Art. 11 (lien ranking after wages), Art. 59 (penalty ceiling, ghost-registration offence, five-year look-back). Any arrears finding should be routed to the closing checklist as a pre-closing deliverable.
- **Computed numbers.** Total employer rate = annuities share for the applicable year (Art. 15 + Decree Third) + 2% (Art. 29) + 0.75% (Decision 1022 Third(6)) for a new-law Saudi employee; 2% only for a non-Saudi; 9% + 2% + legacy SANED for a legacy Saudi employee. The skill must show the formula rows and attach `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` to the rate inputs and `[model knowledge — verify]` to the step month and to the legacy SANED figure, and must apply the SAR 45,000 monthly ceiling and the "basic wage plus Regulation elements" base from Art. 8, stating that the Regulation elements were not read.
- **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a rule that is missing from this file or is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` (registration and payment deadlines, wage elements added by the Regulation, the exact phase-in month, Board decisions under Art. 6, GOSI's SANED coding practice, the penalty regime for legacy contributors), it must say so, cite this file's Open questions, and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must not fill the gap from memory or from the 1421H law by analogy.
# Saudization and the Nitaqat program — التوطين وبرنامج تحفيز المنشآت لتوطين الوظائف (نطاقات)

<!-- Written 2026-09-04. Primary texts read: Labor Law (Arabic, laws.boe.gov.sa) Arts. 26, 28, 30, 32-40, 230-233; HRSD Implementing Regulation of the Labor Law (April 2025 consolidated PDF, Arabic) Arts. 8, 9, 14 and the part-time / flexible-work rules; HRSD "Nitaqat Mutawar Program Procedural Guideline" v2.0 (English, 2021). The 2026-2028 Nitaqat phase, the Qiwa-documentation counting rule, and profession-specific quotas were NOT in the source set and are tagged model knowledge. -->

## Instrument

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
