| Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: `docs/amendment-watch-2026-09-04.md` lead 1.5 claims (i) a Nitaqat 2026-2028 phase effective 26 April 2026 with the "Yellow tier removed" and higher sector quotas, (ii) Saudization credit tied to Qiwa-documented contracts from 15 April 2026, (iii) a revised Labor Law violations and penalties schedule effective 25 February 2026; lead 1.2 claims revised instant-visa quotas for new establishments from 26 August 2026 tied to Nitaqat band. None was confirmed on hrsd.gov.sa or qiwa.sa. Note that the 2021 guide already lists five ranges with no Yellow range, so the "Yellow removed in 2026" claim is either stale or refers to something else |

## Scope

Nitaqat is the mechanism by which HRSD gives effect to the Labor Law's Saudization duty: it classifies every private-sector "entity" (all branches of one firm in one economic activity) into a colour range by comparing its Saudization rate with activity- and size-specific thresholds, and switches ministry services on or off by range (new visas, work-permit issuance and renewal, occupation change, transfer-in of expatriate workers). It applies to every establishment with at least one worker and every economic activity in Annex 1 of the guide. The 75% statutory floor in Labor Law Art. 26(2) is not the operative number: the Minister's reduction power is exercised through the Nitaqat thresholds, which range from under 5% to over 90% depending on activity. Profession-specific Saudization decisions (accounting, engineering, pharmacy, dentistry, sales and customer-service roles, and others) sit alongside Nitaqat and are not in the source set. Domestic workers, government employees, and GCC nationals (who are not "expatriate employees" for the guide's formula) are outside the count.

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Labor Law Art. 26 | All establishments, whatever their activity or headcount, must work to attract and employ Saudis, provide the means for them to stay in work, and give them the opportunity to prove their fitness through guidance, training and qualification. Saudi workers must be not less than 75% of the employer's total workforce; the Minister may temporarily reduce the percentage where technical competencies or academic qualifications are unavailable or posts cannot be filled by nationals. | على جميع المنشآت في مختلف أنشطتها، وأيًّا كان عدد العاملين فيها، العمل على استقطاب السعوديين وتوظيفهم … يجب ألا تقل نسبة العمال السعوديين الذين يستخدمهم صاحب العمل عن 75% من مجموع عماله. وللوزير في حالة عدم توافر الكفايات الفنية أو المؤهلات الدراسية، أو تعذر إشغال الوظائف بالمواطنين أن يخفض هذه النسبة مؤقتًا. | The 75% is the statutory default; every Nitaqat threshold below 75% is an exercise of the Minister's reduction power, which is why HRSD can raise quotas by decision without amending the law. A contract or diligence memo should cite Art. 26(2) as the source of the duty and Nitaqat as the current measure of it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Implementing Regulation Art. 8 (to Art. 26) | In implementation of Art. 26(2), the Saudization ratios establishments must achieve are set through programs the ministry designs, which track the ratio for each private-sector activity and withhold all or some ministry services from establishments that do not achieve the set ratio, "such as the Program for Incentivising Establishments to Localise Jobs (Nitaqat)"; the ministry updates these programs periodically whenever needed. | في تنفيذ أحكام الفقرة (2) من (المادة السادسة والعشرون) من النظام تحدد نسب التوطين لدى المنشآت الواجب تحقيقها من خلال برامج تضعها الوزارة؛ يتم من خلالها متابعة نسب التوطين لكل نشاط من أنشطة القطاع الحساب الخاص وحجب كل أو بعض خدمات الوزارة عن المنشآت التي لا تحقق نسبة التوطين المحددة؛ مثل برنامج تحفيز المنشآت لتوطين الوظائف (نطاقات). | This is the regulatory anchor for Nitaqat and for the service-withholding sanction; it also signals that the program's parameters are administrative and change without notice in the law. Any Nitaqat percentage quoted in a memo must carry a date and the phase it belongs to. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Labor Law Art. 28 (as reworded by M/44 of 1446H) with Implementing Regulation Art. 9 | An employer with 25 or more workers whose work allows it must employ vocationally rehabilitated persons with disabilities at not less than 4% of its workforce, whether through employment channels or otherwise, and must send the ministry a statement of the posts they occupy and their wages (M/44 substituted "channels" for "units", "the ministry" for "the competent labour office", and "persons with disabilities" for the older term; the duty is otherwise unchanged). The Regulation defines disability by a Ministry of Health or public-hospital report or an HRSD identification card, and provides that a person with a disability is counted as more than one worker under Nitaqat only if he holds an HRSD card stating the type and degree of disability and the job matches it; disability may not be a ground to refuse hiring, promotion or training. | على كل صاحب عمل يستخدم خمسة وعشرين عاملاً فأكثر … أن يشغل 4% على الأقل من مجموع عدد عماله من ذوي الإعاقة المؤهلين مهنيًّا، سواء أكان ذلك عن طريق ترشيح قنوات التوظيف أو غيرها. وعليه أن يرسل إلى الوزارة بيانًا بعدد الوظائف والأعمال التي يشغلها ذوو الإعاقة … [اللائحة] يشترط لاحتساب الشخص ذي الإعاقة بأكثر من واحد وفق برنامج تحفيز المنشآت لتوطين الوظائف (نطاقات) … أن يحصل الشخص ذو الإعاقة على بطاقة تعريفية من وزارة الموارد البشرية والتنمية الاجتماعية. | Two separate duties: the 4% disability quota (Art. 28) and the Nitaqat multiplier for disabled Saudi employees (Regulation Art. 9(4)). The multiplier is four (one disabled Saudi counts as four) in HRSD practice `[model knowledge — verify]`; the Regulation confirms only that it is "more than one" and conditions it on the HRSD card and job fit. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 28; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 9 |
| Nitaqat guide, Terms | "Firm": every enterprise managed by a natural or legal person employing one or more employees for a salary of any kind. "Entity": the unit the ministry deals with for calculating Saudization, recruiting expatriates and transferring services; it is all branches of the same economic activity owned by one firm. "Expatriate employee": an employee who is not a GCC citizen. "Saudization rate": total averages of Saudi employees in the entity's branches ÷ (averages of Saudi employees + averages of expatriate employees) × 100. "Range": the entity's classification based on its Saudization rate. | (English guide; HRSD terms: المنشأة، الكيان، العامل الوافد، نسبة التوطين، النطاق) | The denominator is Saudis plus non-GCC expatriates; GCC nationals are outside both numerator and denominator. The rate is computed on "averages", i.e. a rolling average of headcount rather than a point-in-time count (26 weeks in practice `[model knowledge — verify]`), which is why a new hire lifts the rate only gradually. A firm with several activities has one entity per activity; a firm with one activity across many branches is a single entity, so a Red branch cannot be cured by ring-fencing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Nitaqat guide, Technical Details A | Nitaqat Mutawar (a) keeps five ranges: Red, Low Green, Medium Green, High Green and Platinum; (b) uses the consolidated economic activities in Annex 1; (c) abolishes fixed size bands and computes required Saudization from the entity's headcount using fixed values per activity; (d) publishes the coefficients for three years so firms can plan. | (English guide) The Program will gradually increase the Saudization Rate over three years and provide the expected Saudization Rates upfront. 1. Continue using five ranges: Red, Low Green, Medium Green, High Green, and Platinum. | There is no Yellow range in the 2021 design; the amendment-watch lead that "Yellow was removed" in 2026 does not match this text and should be treated as unreliable until HRSD's 2026 material is read. The three-year visibility promise expired with the 2024 coefficients, so the 2026-2028 phase (row below) is a new cycle. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Nitaqat guide, Technical Details B(1)-(3) | The minimum Saudization for each range is y = m × ln(x) + c, where y is the range's minimum rate, m the activity's curve gradient, c the intercept for the activity and year (the third-year value is used in the third year and beyond), x the entity's total workforce, ln the natural logarithm. The formula gives the start of Low Green, Medium Green, High Green and Platinum. Range assignment with n = the entity's rate: Red if n < y(Low Green); Low Green if y(LG) ≤ n < y(MG); Medium Green if y(MG) ≤ n < y(HG); High Green if y(HG) ≤ n < y(Platinum); Platinum if n ≥ y(Platinum). | (English guide) y = m * ln(x) + c … Red: n < Y Low Green; Low Green: Y Low Green <= n < y Medium Green; … Platinum: Y Platinum <= n. | The required rate rises with headcount continuously (no cliff at 500 workers). Worked example in the guide: a manufacturing entity of 400 workers at 35.00% Saudization is High Green in 2023 (thresholds 22.15 / 30.07 / 34.93 / 40.83) and drops to Low Green in 2024 (27.15 / 35.07 / 37.93 / 45.33) with no change in its workforce. A skill may reproduce this arithmetic only with coefficients whose phase and date are stated. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Nitaqat guide, Annex 1 (selected 2024 coefficients) | Coefficients (m; c for Jan 2024 onwards, Low Green / Medium Green / High Green / Platinum): Manufacturing 1.68-2.08; 17.08 / 23.87 / 25.47 / 32.87. Construction and Cleaning −0.37 to 0.00; 12.17 / 14.17 / 17.00 / 22.00. Retail and Wholesale 2.47-2.84; 24.25 / 28.72 / 32.91 / 40.91. Business Services 1.03-2.19; 33.28 / 42.12 / 47.12 / 57.82. Finance 2.60; 50 / 57 / 62 / 65. IT Solutions 2.19-3.22; 26.76 / 32.54 / 41.02 / 48.15. Operations and Maintenance 0.14-0.76; 17.62 / 21.12 / 26.96 / 31.09. Restaurants (excluding fast food) 1.58-1.67; 13.87 / 17.38 / 21.66 / 26.81. Accommodation, Leisure, Tourism 2.42-2.59; 26.10 / 32.52 / 37.90 / 44.02. Medical Labs and Health Services 0.35; 25.24 / 29.74 / 33.24 / 34.24. Employment, Recruitment and Security Services 0.34; 74.50 / 77.50 / 80.50 / 84.50. Combined Entities 2.23; 12.99 / 24.40 / 35.81 / 46. Full table: 30 activities in the guide. | (English guide, Annex No. 1) | These are the last primary-text coefficients available and belong to the phase that ran to 2025. The 2026-2028 phase reportedly raised sector quotas `[model knowledge — verify]`; do not use these numbers to state a target's current range. Use them only to explain the mechanism or to test a target's 2024-2025 historical classification. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` (2023-2024 values; superseded in practice) |
| Nitaqat guide, Services by Range | Platinum, High Green and Medium Green: may apply for new expatriate visas, change expatriate workers' occupations, renew work permits, transfer expatriates' sponsorship into the entity from any other entity, and benefit from immediate counting in Nitaqat. Low Green: cannot apply for new visas or change occupations; can renew existing work permits; immediate counting. Red: cannot change occupations, cannot transfer expatriates in, cannot apply for new visas, cannot issue work permits for new expatriates, cannot renew work permits of existing expatriates. Use of each service is subject to its own regulations. | (English guide) Red Range: Cannot change expatriate workers' occupations. Cannot transfer expatriate workers' sponsorship to the entity. Cannot apply for new expatriate worker visas. Cannot issue work permit for new expatriate workers. Cannot renew work permit of existing expatriate workers. | Red is existential for an expatriate-heavy business: existing work permits lapse at renewal, which makes continued employment of the expatriate unlawful (Labor Law Art. 33) and exposes the employer to per-person penalties under Art. 229 as amended (fine up to SAR 100,000, closure) and the worker to removal through the Ministry of Interior referral in Art. 39 as amended. Low Green freezes growth (no new visas, no occupation change) but keeps the current workforce. "Immediate counting" means a Green-or-better entity gets credit for a new Saudi hire at once, whereas a Red entity is counted on the rolling average only `[model knowledge — verify]` for the mechanics beyond the guide's one-line list. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Implementing Regulation Art. 14 First (recruitment), items 1-7, 12 | Recruitment from abroad may not compete with or displace Saudi labour; an employer's failure to attract Saudis is not a ground for approving recruitment. An establishment wishing to recruit must have achieved the Saudization ratio that enables recruitment under Nitaqat; visas may not be issued if adding them would push the ratio below the ministry's required level; the number of workers an establishment may recruit is set under Nitaqat; recruitment is confined to activities listed in Nitaqat; applications go through the ministry's approved platform. The ministry may refuse recruitment where there is collective delay in paying wages, proven commercial concealment (tasattur), workers left to work for others or for themselves, failure to meet the minimum Saudization ratio, false data (all recruitment suspended up to five years, visas cancelled), sale of visas (five-year suspension), unrenewed work permits or iqamas, or any other case Nitaqat provides. | يجب على المنشأة الراغبة في الاستقدام أن تكون محققة لنسبة التوطين التي تمكنها من الاستقدام المحددة في برنامج تحفيز المنشآت لتوطين الوظائف (نطاقات) … لا يجوز إصدار تأشيرات استقدام تؤدي عند إضافتها إلى العمالة من غير السعوديين الموجودة في المنشأة … إلى تدني نسبة التوطين عن النسبة التي تحددها الوزارة … للوزارة رفض طلب الاستقدام … إذا كانت المنشأة لم تحقق الحد الأدنى من نسبة التوطين المطلوبة. | The regulation turns the Nitaqat band into a legal condition of recruitment, and adds independent refusal grounds (WPS delays, tasattur, unrenewed permits) that bite even in a Green band. For diligence: a target's visa history and any five-year recruitment suspension for false data are discoverable from its Qiwa establishment file `[model knowledge — verify]` and should be requested. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Implementing Regulation Art. 14 Second item 10 and Third item 2(d) | An establishment requesting the transfer of a non-Saudi worker's services to it must have achieved the Saudization ratio required for its activity or entity under Nitaqat. A change of occupation for a non-Saudi worker also requires that the establishment has achieved the required Nitaqat ratio (and is not to a profession reserved for Saudis). | يجب على المنشأة طالبة نقل الخدمات إليها تحقيق نسبة التوطين المطلوبة للنشاط أو للكيان وفق برنامج تحفيز المنشآت لتوطين الوظائف (نطاقات) … [تغيير المهنة] أن تكون المنشأة محققة لنسبة التوطين المطلوبة وفق برنامج تحفيز المنشآت لتوطين الوظائف (نطاقات). | In an acquisition where the buyer entity will take over expatriate staff by transfer of services (rather than by an entity change under Art. 14 Second item 19), the buyer's own Nitaqat band gates the transfer. Plan the receiving entity's band before signing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Implementing Regulation, part-time work rules First(5) and flexible-work rules Second(8) | A Saudi employed by the employer in one of its entities on a part-time contract is counted in Saudization at the rates adopted in the Nitaqat rules, provided he is registered with GOSI as a part-time worker. For flexible-work (hourly) contracts, which are for Saudis only: the establishment earns one full point for every 160 completed flexible-work hours by a worker or group of workers, and the ministry sets how the ratio is computed against total headcount; the flexible-work contract must be documented on the ministry's portal. | يتم احتساب العامل السعودي الذي يعمل لدى صاحب العمل في أحد الكيانات التابعة له بعقد عمل لبعض الوقت في نسب التوطين وفق النسب المعتمدة في قواعد برنامج تحفيز المنشآت لتوطين الوظائف (نطاقات) على أن يتم تسجيله لدى المؤسسة العامة للتأمينات الاجتماعية كعامل لبعض الوقت … يتم احتساب نقطة كاملة للمنشأة عند إكمال ما مجموعه (160) ساعة عمل مرن منجزة. | Part-time Saudis count at a fraction (one half in HRSD practice `[model knowledge — verify]`) and only if GOSI-registered as part-time; flexible-work hours aggregate to whole points at 160 hours. Neither route can substitute for the Qiwa-documented full-time contract in the 2026 counting rule below `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Implementing Regulation, labour-services licensing rules (workers supplied to clients) | Workers whom a licensed labour-services company supplies to a client establishment are included in the workforce on which the client's Saudization ratios are computed, unless the ministry sets specific conditions for counting such workers; licensees must comply with the Saudization requirements the ministry issues for their clients and may not provide services that would breach a client's required ratio. | تدخل العمالة التي تقدم خدماتها لدى عميل المرخص له من المنشآت ضمن العمالة التي تحسب على أساسها نسب التوطين لديه، ما لم تضع الوزارة اشتراطات معينة لاحتساب تلك العمالة في نسب التوطين … | Outsourced expatriate labour (Ajeer-type secondment from a licensed company) does not escape the client's Nitaqat denominator by default. A vendor-agreement review of a manpower contract should ask which entity's Nitaqat file the seconded workers sit on and whether the ministry has set counting conditions for that arrangement. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` |
| Labor Law Art. 229, 230, 231 (as restructured by M/46 of 1436H; Art. 230(5) as amended by M/44 of 1446H) | Without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law, anyone who breaches any provision of the Law, its Regulation or the decisions issued under it is liable to one or more of: a fine not exceeding SAR 100,000; closure of the establishment for up to 30 days; permanent closure. The penalty may be doubled on repetition, and fines multiply by the number of persons concerned (Art. 229). The ministry, by decision of the Minister or his delegate, may itself impose the fine and the temporary closure up to half their maximum, with appeal to the competent administrative court; the Minister issues a schedule of violations and penalties within that half-maximum, graduated by gravity, and a second schedule of violations exceeding half the maximum or warranting permanent closure, which the ministry must take to the competent court; the ministry and the violator may settle by paying the fine the ministry assesses, by decision of the Minister or his delegate (Art. 230). The violator must remove the violation within the period the Regulation sets, failing which it is a new violation (Art. 231). Fines are collected as public funds and go to the Human Resources Development Fund (Art. 232). The original Arts. 230-241 with specific fines per article (SAR 3,000-10,000 for Saudization breaches, etc.) are no longer in force. | يعاقب كل من يخل بأي حكم من أحكام هذا النظام أو لائحته أو القرارات الصادرة تنفيذا له، بعقوبة أو أكثر من العقوبات الآتية: أ – غرامة مالية لا تتجاوز مائة ألف ريـال. ب – إغلاق المنشأة لمدة لا تزيد على ثلاثين يوماً. ج – إغلاق المنشأة نهائيًّا. يجوز مضاعفة العقوبة الموقعة على المخالف في حال تكرار ارتكاب المخالفة. تتعدد الغرامات بتعدد الأشخاص الذين وقعت المخالفة في شأنهم … يصدر – بقرار من الوزير – جدول تُحدد فيه المخالفات والعقوبات المقابلة لها. | The statutory ceiling for a Saudization breach is SAR 100,000 per person concerned, but the amount actually charged comes from the Minister's schedule under Art. 230(2), which HRSD issues by ministerial decision (the current schedule, reportedly revised with effect from 25 February 2026, was not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`). The operative sanction remains the Nitaqat service block (Regulation Art. 8) and, since M/44, refusal to renew work permits under Art. 35 (row above). Fake Saudization (registering Saudis who do not work) is penalised under the HRSD schedule and separately under Social Insurance Law Art. 59(2) (see `social-insurance-law.md`). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Labor Law Arts. 32-34; Art. 35 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H and again by M/44 of 1446H) | Recruitment from abroad requires ministry approval. A non-Saudi may not work, and may not be allowed to work, without a work permit from the ministry, granted only if the worker entered lawfully and is authorised to work, has qualifications or skills the country needs and that nationals lack or lack in sufficient numbers (or is ordinary labour the country needs), and is contracted with and under the responsibility of an employer. Other licences do not substitute for the work permit. Art. 35 in force: the ministry may, for considerations it assesses, refuse to renew a work permit where the employer has breached the job-localisation standards the ministry sets or any other requirements or controls in the Regulation; the Regulation sets procedures ensuring the worker is not harmed by the non-renewal, including the possibility of transferring the worker's services to another employer without the violating employer's consent. (The original Art. 35, requiring a check that no qualified Saudi job-seeker wants the job before renewal, is no longer in force.) | لا يجوز لغير السعودي أن يمارس عملًا، ولا يجوز أن يسمح له بمزاولته، إلا بعد الحصول على رخصة عمل من الوزارة … للوزارة -لاعتبارات تقدرها- أن تمتنع عن تجديد رخصة العمل متى خالف صاحب العمل المعايير الخاصة بتوطين الوظائف التي تضعها الوزارة، وأيًّا من الاشتراطات أو الضوابط الأخرى الواردة في اللائحة. وتبين اللائحة الإجراءات التي تكفل عدم تأثر العامل من عدم تجديد رخصة العمل، بما في ذلك إمكانية نقل خدمة العامل إلى صاحب عمل آخر دون موافقة صاحب العمل المخالف. | Art. 35 as amended is the direct statutory basis for the Nitaqat service block on renewals: a Red entity is denied the Art. 33 permit for new hires and the Art. 35 renewal for existing ones, and the worker may then transfer out without the employer's consent (Regulation Art. 14 Second item 6, see `platform-obligations.md`). The employer therefore loses the workforce, not only the growth headroom, when it falls to Red. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Nitaqat guide, Technical Details (electronic range check) | An entity can identify its range electronically through the ministry's online tool (the guide links to it). | (English guide) An entity can identify its range electronically using the following link. | The tool now sits inside Qiwa as the Nitaqat calculator and establishment dashboard, which shows the current band, the rate, the thresholds for the next band and the number of Saudis needed to move up `[model knowledge — verify]`. A diligence request list should ask for a dated Qiwa Nitaqat screenshot or export for each entity rather than a self-declared percentage. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for the existence of an electronic range check; Qiwa location is `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Counting rules for Saudi employees (HRSD Nitaqat rules, not in the source set) | A Saudi employee counts as one unit if registered with GOSI on a wage of at least SAR 4,000 per month; a wage between SAR 3,000 and 3,999 counts as one half; below SAR 3,000 counts as zero. Additional multipliers and categories in the Nitaqat rules: persons with disabilities (four units, subject to Regulation Art. 9(4)), students on approved programs, released prisoners, Saudi women in certain roles, remote workers, and part-time workers at one half (with GOSI part-time registration). Owners and partners registered on the establishment are counted under separate rules. | (قواعد برنامج نطاقات: احتساب العامل السعودي بأجر مسجل لدى التأمينات لا يقل عن 4000 ريال بوحدة كاملة، ومن 3000 إلى أقل من 4000 بنصف وحدة) | The SAR 4,000 threshold is why the effective minimum wage for Saudis in the private sector is SAR 4,000 even though the Labor Law sets no minimum: below it the employee gives no Nitaqat credit. Offer letters for Saudis at SAR 3,000-3,999 should be flagged in hiring-review. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Qiwa contract documentation as a condition of Nitaqat credit (from 15 April 2026, as claimed) | From 15 April 2026 a Saudi employee is counted in the Nitaqat Saudization rate only if his employment contract is documented (authenticated) on Qiwa; GOSI registration alone no longer earns credit. | (بحسب الإعلان: لا يُحتسب العامل السعودي في نسبة التوطين ما لم يكن عقده موثقاً في منصة قوى) | If confirmed, an entity's Nitaqat rate can fall without any change in headcount simply because contracts were never authenticated on Qiwa. Diligence should reconcile GOSI-registered Saudis with Qiwa-documented contracts. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.5 (eiglaw.com note), unconfirmed on hrsd.gov.sa or qiwa.sa. See `platform-obligations.md` for the underlying documentation duty (Regulation Art. 18(2)), which is settled. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Nitaqat 2026-2028 phase (from 26 April 2026, as claimed) | A new Nitaqat phase for 2026-2028 with higher required Saudization rates by sector, announced by HRSD with a lead time before each annual step; the lead also claims the Yellow band was removed (inconsistent with the 2021 guide, which already had no Yellow band). Entities that were Green under the 2024-2025 coefficients may fall a band on 26 April 2026 without a workforce change, as happened in the guide's own 2023-to-2024 example. | (المرحلة الجديدة من نطاقات 2026-2028 برفع نسب التوطين المطلوبة بحسب النشاط) | Any current-band statement for a target must rest on the Qiwa dashboard after 26 April 2026, not on the 2024 coefficients in this file. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.5 (middleeastbriefing.com), unconfirmed. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Profession-specific Saudization decisions (outside Nitaqat) | HRSD ministerial decisions localise named professions independently of the entity's Nitaqat band, typically in phases: accounting professions 40% in phase 1 (reported from October 2025) rising in later phases to 70%; engineering professions 30% for establishments with five or more engineers (reported from July 2025); pharmacy 35% in community pharmacies and 65% in hospitals; dentistry 45%; technical engineering roles 25%; and earlier decisions on sales outlets, customer-service, consultancy, tourism, and the 100%-Saudi occupations list. Compliance is checked by profession code on Qiwa and non-compliance blocks the affected work permits and attracts the HRSD violations schedule. | (قرارات توطين المهن: المحاسبة 40% في المرحلة الأولى، الهندسة 30% للمنشآت التي لديها خمسة مهندسين فأكثر، الصيدلة 35% و65%، طب الأسنان 45%) | These sit on top of the entity-level Nitaqat requirement; a Platinum entity can still be blocked from renewing an expatriate accountant's permit if its accounting-profession ratio is below the decision's threshold. Percentages and dates here are from memory of 2025 announcements and must be verified against the HRSD decisions before use. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Revised work-visa quotas for new establishments (26 August 2026, as claimed) | Instant-visa caps for newly established businesses tied to company age, participation in the Establishment Programme, and Nitaqat band. | (تعديل حصص التأشيرات الفورية للمنشآت الجديدة بحسب عمر المنشأة والنطاق) | Relevant to hiring-review for a newly incorporated Saudi entity that expects to recruit expatriates in its first year. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.2 (visasupdate.com, low reliability), unconfirmed. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- **Current Nitaqat coefficients.** The only primary text available is the 2021 guide with 2023-2024 values. Obtain HRSD's 2026-2028 phase decision and Annex (Arabic) and replace the Annex 1 row; confirm whether the y = m·ln(x) + c mechanism was retained.
- **The "Yellow band removed" claim.** The 2021 guide already lists five ranges without Yellow. Establish what the April 2026 announcement actually changed.
- **Qiwa-documentation counting rule (15 April 2026).** Confirm the instrument, the effective date, whether it applies to contracts signed before that date, and whether GOSI-registered Saudis without a Qiwa-documented contract are removed from the numerator only or from both numerator and denominator.
- **Counting rules and multipliers** (SAR 4,000 / 3,000 wage thresholds, disability multiplier, students, part-time one-half, remote workers, owners and partners). None was in the source set; the Regulation confirms only the part-time and disability principles.
- **Averaging window** (26 weeks in practice) and what "immediate counting" for Green-and-above entities means operationally.
- **Profession-specific localisation decisions in force in 2026** (accounting, engineering, pharmacy, dentistry, technical roles and others): decision numbers, phases, thresholds and how Qiwa enforces them at permit renewal.
- **Revised penalties schedule (25 February 2026)** and how fake-Saudization penalties interact with Social Insurance Law Art. 59(2).
- **Treatment of GCC nationals, owners/partners, and seconded (Ajeer) workers** in numerator and denominator.
- **Entity grouping for corporate groups**: whether HRSD still permits "combined entities" treatment (Annex 1 lists coefficients for it) and how a change of commercial-register ownership affects the entity's Nitaqat history.

## How skills should use this file

- `hiring-review` (employment-legal), at the eligibility-and-authorisation step: cite Labor Law Arts. 32-35 and the Services-by-Range row before advising that an expatriate hire is feasible; ask for the hiring entity's current Nitaqat band from Qiwa (dated) and refuse to infer it from the Annex 1 coefficients. For a Saudi hire, cite Art. 26 and the counting-rules row (`[model knowledge — verify]`) and flag any Saudi wage below SAR 4,000 as a Nitaqat-credit issue to verify. For an entity under the 2026 Qiwa-documentation rule, point to `platform-obligations.md` for the documentation duty.
- `worker-classification` (employment-legal): cite the labour-services row (seconded workers count in the client's ratio unless the ministry sets conditions) when an outsourcing or secondment structure is being assessed; cite Regulation Art. 14 First item 16 (no recruitment or transfer without actual work) via `platform-obligations.md`.
- `entity-compliance` (corporate-legal): list "Nitaqat band per entity (Qiwa export, dated)", "profession-specific localisation compliance", and "Art. 28 disability quota statement" as periodic checks; cite Regulation Art. 8 for the service-withholding consequence.
- `diligence-issue-extraction` and `tabular-review` (corporate-legal): extract, per Saudi entity, the Nitaqat band and rate, the number of Saudis needed for the next band, any recruitment suspension under Regulation Art. 14 First item 12, and the reconciliation between GOSI Saudis, Qiwa-documented Saudi contracts and payroll. A Red or Low Green band in a target dependent on expatriate work permits is a material finding; cite the Services-by-Range row and Labor Law Art. 33.
- `vendor-agreement-review` (commercial-legal) for manpower and outsourcing contracts: cite the labour-services row and ask for the Nitaqat treatment of supplied workers.
- **Computed numbers.** A skill may apply y = m × ln(x) + c only where it states the phase and source of m and c; using the Annex 1 values in this file yields a 2024-2025 classification and must be labelled as such with `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD] (2023-2024 coefficients, superseded in practice)`. It must never present the result as the entity's current band.
- **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a row that is missing or tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` (current coefficients, counting thresholds, the Qiwa-documentation rule, profession quotas, visa quotas, the averaging window), it must say so, cite the Open questions above, and stop or flag for Saudi counsel rather than fill the gap from memory.
# Occupational safety, work injuries and health services (Labor Law Part VIII and implementing rules) — الوقاية من مخاطر العمل وإصابات العمل والخدمات الصحية (الباب الثامن من نظام العمل)

<!-- Written 2026-09-04 from boe/labor-law-ar.md (Arabic, laws.boe.gov.sa; regenerated 2026-09-04 with [معدلة] markers and the amending blocks in force), labor-en.txt (Bureau of Experts official English), and authority/hrsd-labor-regs-2025-04-ar.txt (HRSD Implementing Regulations, April 2025). Scoped to what a hiring or termination review must check, not to a full HSE audit. -->

## Instrument

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
| Instrument | Labor Law, Part VIII "Protection against occupational hazards, major industrial accidents and work injuries, and health and social services" (Arts. 121–148, plus Art. 131 bis), with the related termination rules (Arts. 80(2), 81(6), 82) and the implementing provisions in the HRSD Regulations (Reg. Arts. 6, 28, 29, 30; Annex 1 Arts. 47–49, 52; Annex 5 cls. 11.5–11.6, 12.5) / نظام العمل، الباب الثامن: الوقاية من مخاطر العمل والوقاية من الحوادث الصناعية الكبرى وإصابات العمل والخدمات الصحية والاجتماعية (المواد 121–148 و131 مكرر) وما يتصل بها من اللائحة التنفيذية |
| Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/51 of 23/8/1426H (Council of Ministers Decision 219 of 22/8/1426H); implementing rules by Ministerial Decision 115921 of 19/8/1446H |
| Issuing authority | Council of Ministers / Royal Decree; Ministry of Human Resources and Social Development for the Regulations and the Minister's decisions under Arts. 121, 131, 131 bis, 141, 144, 147 |
| Effective date | Labor Law: 2006-04-26 (180 days after publication on 25/9/1426H = 28 October 2005, per Art. 245). Part VIII amendments: Art. 137 by M/46 of 5/6/1436H; Art. 131 bis added by M/5 of 7/1/1442H. Regulations: 2025-02-19 (see `labor-law-implementing-regulations.md`). |
| Amendment history | Art. 137: temporary-disability aid raised from 30 days' full wage to 60 days' full wage (M/46 of 1436H). Art. 131 bis: Minister to list dangerous or harmful professions and the categories barred from them (M/5 of 1442H, which also deleted Arts. 149–150 on women's hazardous work). Old Art. 236 (fine SAR 3,000–10,000 and closure for breach of Part VIII Chapters 1–2) was consolidated into the general Art. 229 by M/46 of 1436H. Other Part VIII articles carry no amendment marker on the portal. |
| Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/08381293-6388-48e2-8ad2-a9a700f2aa94/1 ; Regulations: https://www.hrsd.gov.sa/node/838894 |
| Official translation | Bureau of Experts official English translation of the Labor Law (`labor-en.txt`, main text prints the pre-M/46 Art. 137; the M/46 wording is in its appendix) |
| Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 |
| Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: BOE portal shows the Labor Law as "ساري" with 106 articles marked as amended; within Part VIII only Arts. 131 (bis) and 137 are marked [معدلة], both amending blocks read. The HRSD page for the Regulations lists a "لائحة فحوصات اللياقة المهنية والأمراض غير المعدية" (Ministerial Decision 33232 of 11/3/1447H) with a procedural guide; that text was not provided and was not read. No item in `docs/amendment-watch-2026-09-04.md` concerns Part VIII. |

## Scope

Part VIII imposes on every employer a set of workplace safety duties (hygiene, hazard information, personal protective equipment, fire precautions), a matching duty on the worker to use protective means, a special regime for major-hazard establishments, and a compensation regime for work injuries and occupational diseases that applies only where the establishment is not covered by the occupational-hazards branch of the Social Insurance Law (GOSI). It also requires first-aid provision, annual medical examinations for workers exposed to occupational diseases, preventive and curative health care within the cooperative health insurance framework, and welfare provision at remote sites. The Regulations add the work-injury reporting procedure for non-GOSI establishments, the first-aid inventory, the definition of remote locations, and the model work-regulation clauses on health insurance and GOSI registration. The 2025 Regulations extend the safety and work-injury rules to casual, seasonal and temporary workers, trainees and part-timers.

This file does not cover the GOSI occupational-hazards benefits themselves (`social-insurance` reference), the Minister's technical safety standards under Art. 121, the major-hazards regulations under Art. 131, the hazardous-professions list under Art. 131 bis, or the occupational-fitness examination regulation of 1447H — none of which were provided.

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Law Art. 121 | The employer must keep the establishment in a hygienic and clean condition, lit, with potable and washing water, and comply with the other rules, procedures and standards of protection, safety and occupational health that the Minister determines by decision. | على صاحب العمل حفظ المنشأة في حالة صحية ونظيفة، وإنارتها وتأمين المياه الصالحة للشرب والاغتسال، وغير ذلك من قواعد الحماية والسلامة والصحة المهنية وإجراءاتها ومستوياتها وفقاً لما يحدده الوزير بقرار منه. | The technical standards live in ministerial decisions not provided here; a diligence review should ask for the establishment's HRSD/civil-defence safety certificates rather than test against this article alone `[model knowledge — verify]` as to which certificates exist. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 122 | The employer must take the precautions necessary to protect workers from hazards and diseases arising from the work and the machinery used, and to protect and secure the work; must post in a visible place the instructions on work and worker safety in Arabic and, where needed, in any other language the workers understand; and may not charge workers or deduct from their wages any amount for providing this protection. | على كل صاحب عمل أن يتخذ الاحتياطات اللازمة لحماية العمال من الأخطار والأمراض الناجمة عن العمل ... وأن يعلن في مكان ظاهر في المنشأة التعليمات الخاصة بسلامة العمل والعمال، وذلك باللغة العربية وبأي لغة أخرى يفهمها العمال عند الاقتضاء، ولا يجوز لصاحب العمل أن يحمّل العمال أو يقتطع من أجورهم أي مبلغ لقاء توفير هذه الحماية. | Posting is a condition of the Art. 80(2) summary-dismissal ground (breach of "posted" safety instructions); termination-review should ask where and in which languages the instructions were posted. Any PPE charge-back or deduction is void. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 123 | Before the worker starts, the employer must inform him of the hazards of his profession and require him to use the prescribed protective means, and must supply suitable personal protective equipment and train workers in its use. | على صاحب العمل إحاطة العامل قبل مزاولة العمل بمخاطر مهنته، وإلزامه باستعمال وسائل الوقاية المقررة لها، وعليه أن يوفر أدوات الوقاية الشخصية المناسبة للعمال، وتدريبهم على استخدامها. | hiring-review onboarding checklist: documented hazard briefing and PPE training before day one; Annex 5 cl. 12.5 and Annex 1 Art. 48(5) give the employer the right to require pre-employment medical examination for occupational or communicable diseases. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 124 | The worker must use and maintain the protective means for each operation, follow the instructions issued to protect his health and prevent injury and disease, and refrain from any act or omission causing non-implementation of the instructions or misuse or disabling of the means provided to protect the workplace and the health and safety of fellow workers. | على العامل أن يستعمل الوسائل الوقائية المخصصة لكل عملية، وأن يحافظ عليها، وأن ينفذ التعليمات الموضوعة للمحافظة على صحته ووقايته من الإصابات والأمراض. وعليه أن يمتنع عن ارتكاب أي فعل أو تقصير يتسبب عنه عدم تنفيذ التعليمات. | The worker-side duty that underpins disciplinary action for PPE refusal; Annex 1 Art. 48(1) and (4) restate it in the model work regulations. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 125 | The employer must take fire precautions, provide technical fire-fighting means including escape exits kept usable at all times, and post detailed fire-prevention instructions in a visible place. | على صاحب العمل أن يتخذ الاحتياطات اللازمة للوقاية من الحريق، وتهيئة الوسائل الفنية لمكافحته، بما في ذلك تأمين منافذ للنجاة، وجعلها صالحة للاستعمال في أي وقت. | Civil Defence licensing overlaps `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 126 | The employer is liable for emergencies and accidents befalling persons other than his workers who enter the workplace by reason of their function or with the employer's or agents' consent, if caused by neglect of the technical precautions the type of work requires, and must compensate them under the general laws. | صاحب العمل مسؤول عن الطوارئ والحوادث التي يصاب بها أشخاص آخرون غير عماله، ممن يدخلون أماكن العمل بحكم الوظيفة، أو بموافقة صاحب العمل أو وكلائه، إذا كانت بسبب إهمال اتخاذ الاحتياطات الفنية. | Contractor and visitor injuries are a general-law (Civil Transactions Law) tort claim, not a labor claim; the labor courts are not the forum `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Arts. 127–131 and 131 bis | Chapter 2 applies to major-hazard establishments: those that permanently or temporarily produce, process, dispose of, handle, use or store hazardous substances above permitted thresholds; the Ministry sets controls identifying them from a hazardous-substances list; employers determine their status in coordination with the Ministry; the Minister issues regulations on establishment-level arrangements, employer duties, protection of the public and environment, and workers' rights and duties. Art. 131 bis (M/5 of 1442H): the Minister determines by decision the professions and jobs deemed dangerous or harmful and the categories of workers permanently or temporarily barred from them or admitted on conditions, including working-hour limits, in line with the Kingdom's international obligations. | تطبق أحكام هذا الفصل على المنشآت ذات المخاطر الكبرى ... يحدد الوزير - بقرار منه - المهن والأعمال التي تعد خطرة أو ضارة أو من شأنها أن تعرض العامل لأخطار أو أضرار غير عادية، والفئات التي يحظر - دائماً أو مؤقتاً - تشغيلها فيها. | Art. 131 bis replaced the deleted women-specific Arts. 149–150; hiring restrictions by gender now exist only through this ministerial list (not provided). Read from the M/5 amending block in `boe/labor-law-ar.md`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 132 (with Arts. 134, 136) | Chapter 3 (work injuries) does not apply to establishments subject to the occupational-hazards branch of the Social Insurance Law. An injury is a work injury as defined in that Law; occupational diseases are treated as work injuries with the date of first medical observation as the injury date (Art. 134); occupational diseases and degrees of total or partial permanent disability are determined by the GOSI schedules (Art. 136). | لا تسري أحكام هذا الفصل على المنشآت التي تخضع لفرع الأخطار المهنية من نظام التأمينات الاجتماعية. تعد الإصابة إصابة عمل وفق ما هو منصوص عليه في نظام التأمينات الاجتماعية. | Threshold question for every injury review: is the establishment (and the worker) registered in the GOSI occupational-hazards branch? Annex 1 Art. 49 and Annex 5 cl. 11.6 require registration of all workers, so for a compliant private employer Arts. 133–140 are a fallback that bites only where registration was omitted `[model knowledge — verify]` as to how courts allocate liability for an unregistered worker. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 133 | On a work injury or occupational disease the employer must treat the worker and bear all necessary expenses, directly or indirectly, including hospitalisation, medical examinations and tests, radiology, prosthetic devices and transport to treatment. | إذا أصيب العامل بإصابة عمل، أو بمرض مهني، فإن صاحب العمل يلتزم بعلاجه، ويتحمل جميع النفقات اللازمة لذلك ... بما فيها الإقامة في المستشفى، والفحوص والتحاليل الطبية، والأشعة، والأجهزة التعويضية، ونفقات الانتقال إلى أماكن العلاج. | Applies only to non-GOSI establishments (Art. 132); for GOSI-covered workers the occupational-hazards branch pays. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 135 | A relapse or any complication arising from the injury is treated as the injury, with the same aid and treatment rules. | تعد في حكم الإصابة حالة الانتكاس أو أي مضاعفة تنشأ عنها، ويسري عليها بالنسبة للمعونة والعلاج ما يسري على الإصابة الأصلية. | A relapse after termination can revive the employer's Art. 133/137 obligations `[model knowledge — verify]` as to time limits. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 137 (M/46 of 1436H) | Temporary disability from a work injury: financial aid equal to the full wage for sixty days, then 75% of the wage for the whole treatment period; if treatment reaches one year, or it is medically determined that recovery is unlikely and the worker cannot work, the injury is deemed total disability, the contract is terminated and the worker is compensated for the injury; the employer may not recover what was paid during that year. | للمصاب - في حالة عجزه المؤقت عن العمل الناتج من إصابة عمل - الحق في معونة مالية تعادل أجره كاملاً لمدة ستين يوماً ثم يستحق مقابلاً مالياً يعادل (75%) من أجره طوال المدة التي يستغرقها علاجه. فإذا بلغت مدة العلاج سنة أو تقرر طبياً عدم احتمال شفائه ... عُدت الإصابة عجزاً كلياً، وينهي العقد ويعوض عن الإصابة. | The original text of the [معدلة] article in `boe/labor-law-ar.md` and the BOE English main text print thirty days; the sixty-day figure is the M/46 amending block (and the English appendix). Termination on total disability is a lawful ground distinct from Art. 82 (no termination for ordinary sickness until sick leave is exhausted). termination-review must not treat a work-injury absence as sick leave under Art. 117. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 138 | Permanent total disability or death: compensation equal to the wage for three years, minimum SAR 54,000, to the worker or his beneficiaries. Permanent partial disability: the disability percentage under the approved guide multiplied by the total-disability compensation. | إذا نتج عن الإصابة عجز دائم كلي، أو أدت الإصابة إلى وفاة المصاب، فللمصاب أو المستحقين عنه الحق في تعويض يقدر بما يعادل أجره عن مدة ثلاث سنوات بحد أدنى قدره أربعة وخمسون ألف ريال. أما إذا نتج عن الإصابة عجز دائم جزئي، فإن المصاب يستحق تعويضاً معادلاً لنسبة ذلك العجز المقدر ... مضروبة في قيمة تعويض العجز الدائم الكلي. | Formula for non-GOSI cases: max(36 × monthly wage, 54,000) for total disability or death; × disability percentage for partial. Paid in addition to the end-of-service award `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 139 | The employer is not bound by Arts. 133, 137 and 138 if it is established that the worker deliberately injured himself, the injury resulted from the worker's intentional misconduct, or the worker refused without legitimate reason to see a physician or to accept treatment by the physician the employer designated. | لا يلزم صاحب العمل بما ورد في المواد الثالثة والثلاثين بعد المائة والسابعة والثلاثين بعد المائة والثامنة والثلاثين بعد المائة إذا ثبت: أن العامل تعمد إصابة نفسه؛ أن الإصابة حدثت بسبب سوء سلوك مقصود من جانب العامل؛ أن العامل امتنع عن عرض نفسه على طبيب. | The burden is on the employer ("إذا ثبت"); an internal-investigation file on the incident is the evidence. Negligence short of intentional misconduct does not disqualify. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 140 | Liability of previous employers for an occupational disease is determined on the treating physician's report; they owe the Art. 138 compensation in proportion to the period the worker served each, provided their industries or occupations are of a kind that causes the disease. | تحدد مسؤولية أصحاب العمل السابقين الذين اشتغل لديهم العامل المصاب بالمرض المهني على ضوء التقرير الطبي للطبيب المعالج، ويلزم هؤلاء بالتعويض ... كل بنسبة المدة التي قضاها المصاب في خدمته. | A latent occupational-disease liability survives termination and follows the employment history; relevant to M&A diligence on industrial targets. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Law Art. 141 and Reg. Art. 28 | Work-injury reporting procedures are set by ministerial decision. The Regulations: where the establishment is not in the GOSI occupational-hazards branch, the employer notifies the competent labor office within one week of learning of the injury; the office determines whether it is a work injury on the official reports, refers the worker to a government hospital for a disability report if none exists or either party contests it, fixes the compensation from the disability percentage, and refers a refusal by either party to the labor courts. | يقوم صاحب العمل بإبلاغ مكتب العمل المختص عن إصابة عامل لديه خلال أسبوع من تاريخ علمه بها ... يقوم مكتب العمل بتحديد التعويض المستحق للمصاب وفقاً لنسبة العجز الواردة بالتقرير الطبي. إذا رفض أحد الطرفين ما حدده مكتب العمل يحال الموضوع إلى المحاكم العمالية المختصة. | For GOSI-covered establishments the GOSI notification rules and deadlines apply instead `[model knowledge — verify]`. A missed one-week report is a scheduled violation `[model knowledge — verify]` as to amount. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` (Reg. Art. 28: `[authority — HRSD]`) |
| Law Art. 142 and Reg. Art. 29 | Every employer must keep one or more first-aid cabinets stocked with medicines and first-aid necessities; the Regulations list the minimum inventory (burn gauze 50, sponge dressings 10, cleaning gauze 50, sterile 10×10 gauze 50, 5×5 gauze 50, 5×5 gauze bandages 10, tourniquet 1, pressure bandages 10, medical swabs 100, plaster rolls 5, sterile plasters 20, sterile gloves 20, masks 10, eye-wash 1, folding stretcher 1, wound antiseptic 1, airway kit 1, thigh/leg/forearm splint kit 1, spinal board 1, cervical collar kit 1, scissors 2, large blanket 1, forceps 2), require continuous usability and replenishment, a suitable, hygienic and temperature-appropriate cabinet marked with a red crescent on a white background with posted location and responsible person, and supplementation according to the nature and size of the work. | على كل صاحب عمل أن يعد خزانة أو أكثر للإسعافات الطبية ... يجب أن تحتوي خزانة الإسعافات على المستلزمات التالية ... وأن يتم تمييزها بهلال أحمر على خلفية بيضاء، وتعلق إعلانات بشكل ظاهر في أماكن العمل تدل على مكان الخزانة، واسم المسؤول عنها. | Inspection checklist item; the person responsible for first aid must be named. The Law also leaves the qualifications of first-aid providers to the Regulations, which the 2025 text does not specify beyond naming a responsible person. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` (Law Art. 142: `[BOE — Arabic]`) |
| Law Art. 143 and Reg. Art. 5(6) | The employer must have one or more physicians examine, comprehensively and at least once a year, workers exposed to any occupational disease in the GOSI schedules, and record the results in its records and the workers' files; the Regulations require a medical-examination register in establishments where occupational disease is possible, listing examined workers, dates and results. | على كل صاحب عمل أن يعهد إلى طبيب أو أكثر بفحص عماله المعرضين لاحتمال الإصابة بأحد الأمراض المهنية ... فحصاً شاملاً مرة كل سنة على الأقل، وأن يثبت نتيجة ذلك الفحص في سجلاته، وكذلك في ملفات أولئك العمال. | Annual examinations and the register are what a termination-review checks before accepting "unfit for work" as a ground; the 1447H occupational-fitness examination regulation (Decision 33232 of 11/3/1447H) is listed on the HRSD page but was not read `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` (Reg. Art. 5: `[authority — HRSD]`) |
| Law Art. 144, Annex 1 Art. 49, Annex 5 cls. 11.5–11.6 | The employer must provide preventive and curative health care to the standards the Minister sets, taking account of the Cooperative Health Insurance Law; the model work regulations require the establishment to insure all workers under the Cooperative Health Insurance Law and to register all workers in the GOSI occupational-hazards branch; the unified contract obliges the employer to provide health care per the Cooperative Health Insurance Law and to register the worker with GOSI and pay contributions. | تقوم المنشأة بالتأمين على جميع العاملين لديها صحياً؛ وفقاً لما يقرره نظام التأمين الصحي التعاوني ولائحته التنفيذية، كما تقوم بالاشتراك عن جميع العاملين في فرع الأخطار المهنية لدى المؤسسة العامة للتأمينات الاجتماعية. | Health insurance and GOSI registration are contract obligations, so their absence is both a regulatory breach and a contractual breach that can ground the worker's Art. 81(1) exit (employer's failure of essential statutory obligations) `[model knowledge — verify]` as to court practice. Reg. Art. 6 forbids retaining the worker's medical-insurance card. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` (Law Art. 144: `[BOE — Arabic]`) |
| Law Arts. 146–148 and Reg. Art. 30 | At remote locations (more than 50 km by paved road or 25 km by unpaved road from the urban boundary, or settlements without utilities) the employer must, as the Minister determines, provide at its cost shops at moderate prices, recreation and sports facilities, medical arrangements including comprehensive treatment of the worker's resident family, schools for children where lacking, mosques or prayer areas, and literacy programs (Art. 146); at remote locations, mines, quarries and oil-exploration sites, housing, camps and meals on conditions and charges the Minister sets (Art. 147); and daily transport from residence or an assembly point where regular transport does not reach the workplace at working times (Art. 148). | يلزم صاحب العمل وعلى نفقته بالنسبة لمن يؤدي عملاً في الأماكن البعيدة عن العمران ... تحدد الأماكن البعيدة عن العمران: مواقع العمل التي تبتعد عن النطاق العمراني ... بمسافة تزيد على خمسين كيلو متراً إذا كانت الطريق معبدة؛ ومسافة خمسة وعشرين كيلو متراً إذا كانت الطريق غير معبدة. | For project and construction hires the camp and meal obligations are part of the wage-and-benefits picture; Art. 105 also allows accumulated weekly rest at remote sites (up to eight weeks) with worker and Ministry agreement (read from BOE). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` (Reg. Art. 30: `[authority — HRSD]`) |
| Law Art. 80(2) (M/46) and Art. 81(6) | Summary dismissal without award, notice or compensation is available where the worker, despite a written warning, deliberately disregards the safety instructions for work and workers that the employer posted in a visible place (Art. 80(2)); the worker may leave without notice keeping all statutory rights where the workplace has a serious hazard threatening his safety or health of which the employer knew and took no steps indicating its removal (Art. 81(6)). | إذا لم يؤدِّ العامل التزاماته الجوهرية ... أو لم يراعِ عمداً التعليمات - التي أعلن عنها صاحب العمل في مكان ظاهر - الخاصة بسلامة العمل والعمال رغم إنذاره كتابة ... إذا كان في مقر العمل خطر جسيم يهدد سلامة العامل أو صحته، بشرط أن يكون صاحب العمل قد علم بوجوده، ولم يتخذ من الإجراءات ما يدل على إزالته. | Both grounds require documentary proof (posting, written warning, knowledge of the hazard). Art. 80 as amended read from the M/46 amending block in `boe/labor-law-ar.md`; Art. 81 carries no [معدلة] marker. Art. 82 (no termination for illness before sick leave is exhausted) applies to ordinary illness; work-injury absence is governed by Art. 137. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |
| Reg. Art. 6 (Law Art. 6), Law Art. 49, Law Art. 120 | Casual, seasonal and temporary workers are subject to the safety rules, occupational health, work injuries and compensation provisions; training and qualification contracts are subject to the safety and health rules and the work-injury provisions (Art. 49); part-time workers are outside the Law except as the Minister decides, save that they keep the protection of comparable full-time workers in occupational safety and health and work injuries (Art. 120). | تسري على العامل العرضي والموسمي والمؤقت الأحكام الخاصة ... وقواعد السلامة والصحة المهنية وإصابات العمل والتعويض عنها ... لا تسري أحكام هذا النظام [على العمل لبعض الوقت] إلا في الحدود التي يقررها الوزير، باستثناء الحماية الممنوحة للعاملين طوال الوقت المماثلين، من حيث السلامة والصحة المهنية وإصابات العمل. | Safety and injury protection is the one part of the Law that reaches every category of worker, including interns and part-timers; worker-classification cannot use "casual" or "part-time" to exclude injury liability. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` (Law Arts. 49, 120: `[BOE — Arabic]`) |
| Law Art. 229 (M/46 of 1436H), replacing old Art. 236 | Any breach of the Law, Regulations or implementing decisions is punishable by a fine up to SAR 100,000, closure up to 30 days, or permanent closure, singly or combined; doubled on repetition; fines multiplied by the number of persons concerned. The original Art. 236 (fine SAR 3,000–10,000 per violation and/or closure, with suspension of the hazard source, for breach of Part VIII Chapters 1–2 and the Art. 121 rules) was consolidated into this article. | مع عدم الإخلال بأي عقوبة أشد ينص عليها نظام آخر، يعاقب كل من يخل بأي حكم من أحكام هذا النظام أو لائحته أو القرارات الصادرة تنفيذاً له، بعقوبة أو أكثر من العقوبات الآتية: غرامة مالية لا تتجاوز مائة ألف ريال؛ إغلاق المنشأة لمدة لا تزيد على ثلاثين يوماً؛ إغلاق المنشأة نهائياً. | The specific safety-violation fines sit in the ministerial schedule (not provided); closure is in principle available for safety breaches. Read from the M/46 amending block in `boe/labor-law-ar.md`; the original text of Arts. 229–241 is the pre-M/46 penalty scheme. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- Which Minister's decisions currently set the Art. 121 technical standards, the Art. 131 major-hazard regulations and the Art. 131 bis hazardous-professions list, and whether any of them restricts hiring by gender or age beyond Reg. Arts. 32–35.
- The occupational-fitness examinations regulation (Ministerial Decision 33232 of 11/3/1447H) listed on the HRSD page: scope (which professions require pre-employment or periodic fitness examinations), and its interaction with Art. 143 and with Annex 5 cl. 12.5.
- GOSI occupational-hazards branch: reporting deadline, benefit levels and how liability is allocated where a worker should have been registered but was not (does the employer owe Arts. 133–138 amounts, GOSI-equivalent benefits, or both).
- Whether Art. 138 compensation is cumulative with the end-of-service award and with Art. 77 damages where the Art. 137 termination is contested.
- Time limit for Art. 135 relapse claims after termination, given the 12-month limitation in Art. 234.
- Current schedule amounts for safety violations (posting, PPE, first-aid cabinet, missed injury report) and whether closure has been applied in practice.

## How skills should use this file

- **hiring-review** loads this file at the onboarding-checklist step and must cite Law Art. 123 (hazard briefing and PPE before start), Annex 5 cls. 11.5–11.6 and Annex 1 Art. 49 (health insurance and GOSI registration as contract terms), Annex 5 cl. 12.5 (medical examination right), Reg. Art. 6 / Law Arts. 49, 120 (safety protection for casual, trainee and part-time hires), and Law Art. 131 bis (hazardous professions list — stop and flag, list not provided).
- **termination-review** loads at the grounds step for any dismissal citing safety misconduct or medical unfitness and must cite Law Art. 80(2) (posting, written warning, deliberate breach), Art. 122 (posting requirement), Art. 81(6) (worker's exit for known hazard), Art. 137 (work-injury absence and total-disability termination, sixty days full wage then 75%), Art. 82 (no termination for ordinary sickness until leave exhausted), Art. 139 (employer's burden to prove exclusions), Art. 143 (examination records). Computed injury amounts (non-GOSI only): temporary disability = 60 days × daily wage + 75% × daily wage × remaining treatment days up to one year; permanent total or death = max(36 × monthly wage, SAR 54,000); partial = that figure × disability percentage; the skill must first confirm the establishment is outside the GOSI occupational-hazards branch (Art. 132) and tag the GOSI determination `[model knowledge — verify]`.
- **internal-investigation** loads for accident and safety-breach investigations and must record the facts that Arts. 80(2), 139 and 141 / Reg. Art. 28 make decisive: date of knowledge (one-week report clock), posted instructions, written warning, intentional misconduct, refusal of treatment.
- **worker-classification** loads at the exclusions step and must cite Reg. Art. 6 and Law Arts. 49, 120 to reject any classification argument that removes safety or injury protection.
- **policy-drafting** and **handbook-updates** load when drafting HSE, PPE, incident-reporting or fitness-for-duty sections and must cite Arts. 122–125, 142–143, Reg. Art. 29 (inventory and signage) and Annex 1 Arts. 47–49.
- **Stop rule:** where a skill needs the ministerial safety standards, the hazardous-professions list, the fitness-examination regulation, the GOSI reporting rules, or any fine amount — all tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` or absent here — it must say so and stop or flag for Saudi counsel rather than supply the content from memory. Where a row notes that an article is marked [معدلة] in `boe/labor-law-ar.md` (Arts. 137, 80, 229, 131 bis), the skill must cite the amended version stated in the row, never the original text printed above the amending blocks.

